/* ================= QUESTION BANK — Reading & Writing (part 1) =================
   All passages and items are original, written in the style and difficulty bands of the
   Digital SAT. tier: core / advanced / elite (elite = 700+ band). d = domain code. */
window.RW1 = [
/* ---------- Craft and Structure ---------- */
{id:"CS01", d:"CS", tier:"elite",
 p:"In her surveys of tidal marshes, ecologist Amara Osei grew wary of the word “pristine.” Cores pulled from even the remotest marshes bore measurable traces of nitrogen fertilizer, delivered by wind and rain rather than by any nearby farm. The label “untouched,” she argued, was not merely imprecise but <span class='ul'>pernicious</span>: it implied an absence of human influence that atmospheric deposition had long since made untenable — and, worse, it shaped which wetlands regulators chose to fund and protect.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “pernicious” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["Destructive in a way that is easy to overlook","Old-fashioned and rarely used","Brief and to the point","Welcoming and hospitable"], a:0,
 e:"The passage gives “pernicious” a consequence: the mislabeling actively steers protection money toward the wrong wetlands (“it shaped which wetlands regulators chose to fund”). That is harm — harm delivered subtly, through a word that sounds harmless. “Old-fashioned,” “brief,” and “hospitable” are all positive or neutral descriptors that ignore the causal clue “worse, it shaped….”"},
{id:"CS02", d:"CS", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Early quantum theorists described electron orbits as “stationary,” a term that confuses students to this day: the particles occupying those orbits are anything but motionless. The word referred instead to the orbits’ remarkable <span class='ul'>constancy</span> — electrons in them neither radiate energy away nor spiral inward, in flat contradiction of what classical electromagnetism predicted.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “constancy” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["Freedom from change or decay","Ability to conduct electricity","Tendency to vary unpredictably","Capacity to emit light"], a:0,
 e:"The passage defines the term immediately after the dash: electrons “neither radiate energy away nor spiral inward.” That is stability over time, i.e., freedom from change. The distractors hijack nearby physics vocabulary (conduction, variation, light emission) without matching the defining clause."},
{id:"CS03", d:"CS", tier:"elite",
 p:"For the Victorian essayist, “sympathy” did not denote mere pity; it named an act of disciplined imagination in which the reader <span class='ul'>inhabited</span> the circumstances of a stranger — feeling their weight, so to speak — before presuming to pass judgment on them.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “inhabited” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["Imaginatively occupied the position of","Briefly visited the home of","Publicly mocked","Formally measured and recorded"], a:0,
 e:"The sentence redefines “sympathy” as imagination: the reader mentally lives inside a stranger’s circumstances (“feeling their weight”). “Inhabited” is metaphorical, not literal — option B traps readers who take the word physically, while “mocked” and “measured” contradict the empathetic framing of the whole sentence."},
{id:"CS04", d:"CS", tier:"elite",
 p:"Historians have long treated the 1713 Treaty of Utrecht as a straightforward settlement, the moment when European naval rivalry finally cooled. But shipping manifests from Bristol tell a different story: after 1713, voyages to Atlantic trading posts multiplied, and cargo insurance rates on those routes climbed to record highs. Competition, the manifests suggest, did not end — it emigrated, relocating from European waters to the far Atlantic, where it accelerated.",
 q:"Which choice best states the function of the shipping-manifest evidence in the text?",
 ch:["It supplies the concrete basis for the author’s revision of the standard interpretation","It illustrates why Bristol’s archives are more reliable than naval records","It explains why historians originally ignored the treaty","It confirms that rivalry between European navies ended abruptly in 1713"], a:0,
 e:"The manifests are the “But” evidence — voyage counts and insurance rates — that drives the reinterpretation (“Competition… did not end — it emigrated”). Option B invents a comparison between archive types the text never makes; C invents a claim about historians’ motives; D flatly contradicts the text’s thesis."},
{id:"CS05", d:"CS", tier:"advanced",
 p:"When honeybees locate nectar, they return to the hive and perform the famous “waggle dance.” What the dance means was once fiercely contested: ethologists led by Karl von Frisch argued that the dance encodes the direction and distance of the food, while skeptics countered that it merely rouses hive-mates to search on their own. Resolution came from an unlikely performer — a robotic bee, built by researchers, whose programmed dance sent recruits flying to exactly the locations the robot “advertised.”",
 q:"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?",
 ch:["A behavior is described, two rival explanations are weighed, and experimental evidence favoring one is reported","A scientific controversy is introduced, the lead researcher’s career is summarized, and the controversy is declared unresolvable","A puzzling observation is presented, existing research methods are criticized, and new funding is called for","A bold claim is made, two exceptions are acknowledged, and the claim is withdrawn"], a:0,
 e:"The text moves in exactly that order: waggle dance (behavior) → von Frisch vs. skeptics (two rivals) → robotic-bee result sending recruits to “exactly the locations the robot ‘advertised’” (evidence favoring the encoding view). No career summary, no method critique, and no withdrawal appears."},
{id:"CS06", d:"CS", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Textile historians once assumed that industrial mechanization spread its benefits evenly across a region’s workforce. The wage ledgers of the Lancashire mills, however, <span class='ul'>complicate</span> that narrative: while cloth output doubled between 1820 and 1840, piece rates paid to weavers steadily fell, and family members increasingly worked at home to make up the difference.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “complicate” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["Cast doubt on the adequacy of a simple account","Make a task more physically difficult","Entangle threads during production","Increase the cost of manufacturing"], a:0,
 e:"“Complicate” operates on the historical narrative, not on a physical task: the ledgers undercut the “even benefits” story by showing falling pay amid rising output. Option A captures that sense of undermining a tidy account; the other options wrongly drag the word into its literal or industrial senses."},
{id:"CS07", d:"CS", tier:"elite", pair:true,
 p:"<b>Text 1</b><br>After wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone in 1995, willows along streams grew dramatically taller within a decade. Biologist L. Ramsey (2020) credits a “trophic cascade”: fear of wolves changed elk browsing behavior, keeping elk away from streambanks and letting vegetation rebound.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>Wolf absence cannot explain the willow boom, argues ecologist P. Devan (2022): archival photographs show willow recovery beginning before 1995, and the timing instead matches a surge in beaver dam building, which raised water tables along the same streams. Attribution to wolves, Devan writes, mistakes coincidence for cause.",
 q:"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Ramsey’s claim in Text 1?",
 ch:["By arguing that the willow recovery predates the wolves’ return and so requires a different explanation","By conceding that wolves alone drove the willow recovery","By denying that willows along streams recovered at all","By asserting that elk populations were unaffected by either wolves or beavers"], a:0,
 e:"Text 2 accepts that willows recovered but disputes the cause and the timeline: recovery “begin[s] before 1995,” so wolves cannot be the driver, and beaver dams are proposed instead. B is Text 1’s own view, C contradicts both texts, and D is never claimed by anyone."},
{id:"CS08", d:"CS", tier:"advanced", pair:true,
 p:"<b>Text 1</b><br>After wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone in 1995, willows along streams grew dramatically taller within a decade. Biologist L. Ramsey (2020) credits a “trophic cascade”: fear of wolves changed elk browsing behavior, keeping elk away from streambanks and letting vegetation rebound.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>Wolf absence cannot explain the willow boom, argues ecologist P. Devan (2022): archival photographs show willow recovery beginning before 1995, and the timing instead matches a surge in beaver dam building, which raised water tables along the same streams. Attribution to wolves, Devan writes, mistakes coincidence for cause.",
 q:"Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?",
 ch:["Text 2 challenges the causal explanation offered in Text 1 by questioning its timing","Text 2 confirms the finding in Text 1 and proposes extending it to other parks","Text 1 questions the value of the photographic evidence described in Text 2","The two texts describe unrelated ecosystems that share no observable variables"], a:0,
 e:"Both texts address the same willow recovery; they diverge on why it happened. Text 2’s pre-1995 photographs directly attack the timing assumption beneath Text 1’s wolf explanation. Option C reverses the direction of the dispute, and B and D describe relationships the texts simply do not have."},
{id:"CS09", d:"CS", tier:"elite",
 p:"The novelist’s late style is often called “austere,” yet the spareness is deceptive: each clause bears the weight of a paragraph, and what readers mistake for plainness is really <span class='ul'>stringent</span> compression — nothing survives the final draft that has not earned its place twice over.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “stringent” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["Rigorously demanding","Casual and careless","Warmly generous","Unnecessarily long"], a:0,
 e:"“Stringent” describes the compression: a strict, ruthless editing standard in which every clause must “earn its place twice.” “Casual” and “unnecessarily long” contradict the discipline the passage praises; “generously” has nothing anchoring it in the text."},

/* ---------- Information and Ideas ---------- */
{id:"II01", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"Reef scientists have mapped “thermal refugia”: patches where cooler, upwelled water buffers corals during marine heatwaves. A ten-year survey across sixty-three sites found that corals in refugia bleached far less often than their neighbors — but, unexpectedly, when refugia corals did bleach, they recovered more slowly, perhaps because consistently cool water supports fewer of the symbiont varieties that accelerate repair after heat stress.",
 q:"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?",
 ch:["Refugia reduce how often corals bleach but appear to carry a hidden cost: slower recovery when bleaching does occur","Refugia should be eliminated because they weaken corals’ symbiont diversity","Bleaching is caused primarily by a shortage of symbiont varieties","Refugia corals bleach more frequently but recover faster than other corals"], a:0,
 e:"The text’s arc is benefit + unexpected trade-off: less frequent bleaching, slower recovery when it happens. Option B overreaches (“eliminated” is never suggested), C misreads the symbiont point as a cause of bleaching rather than of slow recovery, and D inverts every comparison in the passage."},
{id:"II02", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Before 1840, abolitionist women coordinated nationwide petition drives through little more than correspondence: letters, reprinted extracts, and borrowed mailing lists. The network had no headquarters and no single leader — the very features that made contemporaries dismiss it as amateurish. Yet those features were its armor: because no central committee existed, the arrest or intimidation of any one organizer left the rest of the network, and its mail, moving.",
 q:"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?",
 ch:["The network’s lack of central organization, though dismissed at the time, helped it withstand repression","The network collapsed after 1840 because it lacked a headquarters","Contemporaries secretly funded the network’s correspondence operations","Letter networks were more effective than newspapers at spreading abolitionism"], a:0,
 e:"The pivot “Yet those features were its armor” states the thesis: decentralization, mocked at the time, is what made repression ineffective. B contradicts the text (the network kept moving), C invents secret funding, and D makes an unsupported comparison to newspapers."},
{id:"II03", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"Mara had rehearsed the apology twice on the stairwell, in the calm voice she saved for difficult rooms. But when the door opened she only held out the parcel. Some sentences, she had learned, survive better unspoken; gratitude audited aloud has a way of curdling into debt.",
 q:"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?",
 ch:["Mara offers a gift rather than words because speaking the feeling risks diminishing it","Mara forgot the apology once the door opened","The recipient refused to accept the parcel","Mara believes all spoken apologies are insincere"], a:0,
 e:"The final sentence is the key: feelings “audited aloud… curdle into debt,” so she substitutes the parcel for the rehearsed speech. B is contradicted by the rehearsal; C never happens in the text; D overgeneralizes from one calculated choice to a universal belief."},
{id:"II04", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The 1903 logbook of the Dingman textile mill records wages of $4.20 for a standard fifty-eight-hour week. In the same year, a municipal cost-of-living report placed a single adult worker’s minimum weekly expenses — board, fuel, and lodging — at $4.90.",
 q:"Which choice can most reasonably be inferred from the text?",
 ch:["A single worker paid solely the logged wage could not cover the minimum weekly expenses described in the municipal report","The municipal report overstated minimum expenses to justify wage cuts","Most Dingman workers worked fewer than fifty-eight hours per week","Board and lodging at the mill were free for employees"], a:0,
 e:"Put the two figures together: $4.20 < $4.90, so the wage alone falls short of the report’s minimum by $0.70. The comparison is exactly the one the text sets up. The other options invent motives (B), contradict the logged hours (C), or add unstated benefits (D)."},
{id:"II05", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"Every review praised the string quartet’s “restraint,” and violinist Hana Kessler suspected the word had simply followed the ensemble home. At their next concert the players offered nothing but late Beethoven — repertoire the same critics routinely called volcanic — and yet the morning reviews arrived wearing the identical adjective once again.",
 q:"Which choice can most reasonably be inferred from the text?",
 ch:["Kessler believes the reviewers apply “restraint” out of habit rather than in response to what is played","The quartet deliberately played quieter than usual at the second concert","Late Beethoven is universally considered restrained repertoire","The quartet was hired by the newspaper to write its own reviews"], a:0,
 e:"Kessler’s suspicion is confirmed by the experiment: wildly contrasting programs, identical adjective. The reasonable inference is that the label is automatic — the word “followed the ensemble home.” B contradicts the volcanic program, C contradicts “the same critics routinely called [it] volcanic,” and D is pure invention."},
{id:"II06", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Radio-collared fruit bats in a fragmented rainforest foraged longest in open pasture on dark nights, but on bright-moon nights nearly every tracked individual cut its trips short and returned to the roost before midnight, sitting motionless at the entrance until the light dimmed.",
 q:"Which choice can most reasonably be inferred from the text?",
 ch:["Moonlight suppresses the bats’ willingness to remain outside foraging","Bats cannot see at all on bright nights","Pasture contains more food than the rainforest interior","The bats’ collars increased their energy needs"], a:0,
 e:"The only variable that changes alongside the behavior is moonlight: dark nights, long trips; bright nights, early returns and waiting. Inferring moonlight’s suppressive effect is direct. “Cannot see at all” overstates, pasture food supply and collar effects are never touched."},
{id:"II07", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"Biologists proposed that some moths evolved ultrasonic hearing to detect the echolocation calls of pursuing bats. The hypothesis fits mainland populations, where moths dive when bats call. It faces a puzzle, however, on a certain bat-free island: the moths there possess fully functional ears tuned to the same ultrasonic frequencies, although no bat has ever been recorded on the island.",
 q:"Which finding, if true, would most directly challenge the bat-detection hypothesis as an explanation for the island moths’ hearing?",
 ch:["The island moths’ ears are most sensitive precisely to the frequencies produced by the parasitoid wasps that hunt them there","Mainland moths dive in response to recorded bat calls","Bat echolocation on the mainland overlaps the frequencies moths can hear","The island’s wasp populations are smaller than the mainland’s"], a:0,
 e:"To challenge the “hearing evolved for bats” story on a bat-free island, you need evidence of a different function operating there. Option A supplies exactly that: tuning matched to the island’s real predator (wasps). B and C support the mainland hypothesis without touching the island puzzle, and D is irrelevant to why the ears exist."},
{id:"II08", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Transit planners claimed that the redesigned express bus route “spread its benefits across the whole city.” Ridership changes in the first year after the redesign are shown below.<br><br><table style='width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.9rem'><tr style='color:var(--muted)'><th style='text-align:left;padding:4px'>District</th><th style='text-align:right;padding:4px'>Change in weekly boardings</th></tr><tr><td style='padding:4px'>North</td><td style='text-align:right;padding:4px'>+4%</td></tr><tr><td style='padding:4px'>East</td><td style='text-align:right;padding:4px'>+6%</td></tr><tr><td style='padding:4px;background:var(--card2)'>South</td><td style='text-align:right;padding:4px;background:var(--card2)'>+31%</td></tr><tr><td style='padding:4px'>West</td><td style='text-align:right;padding:4px'>+2%</td></tr></table>",
 q:"Which choice best describes data from the table that most directly undermines the planners’ claim?",
 ch:["The South’s +31% dwarfs the +2% to +6% changes elsewhere, showing the benefits concentrated in one part of the city","Ridership rose in every district, confirming citywide improvement","The West’s +2% proves the route should be cancelled","The North and East grew more slowly than the city’s population"], a:0,
 e:"To undermine “spread across the whole city,” you need evidence of uneven distribution — the South’s 31% against everyone else’s low single digits does that directly. Option B actually supports the planners, C draws an unsupported conclusion from one number, and D imports population data the table does not contain."},
{id:"II09", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"Aunt Inés kept the shop’s radio tuned to a station whose signal dissolved by noon. The landlord always came on the first of the month; on those mornings Inés raised the volume until the shelves hummed, counted the rent into his palm without once looking up, and afterwards left the radio on for the rest of the day, though no one could hear it.",
 q:"Which quotation from the text best supports the inference that Inés found rent days unpleasant?",
 ch:["“raised the volume until the shelves hummed, counted the rent into his palm without once looking up”","“kept the shop’s radio tuned to a station whose signal dissolved by noon”","“the landlord always came on the first of the month”","“afterwards left the radio on for the rest of the day”"], a:0,
 e:"Only option A shows the behavior on rent days themselves: maximal noise and deliberate refusal of eye contact — the body language of avoiding an encounter. B and C are neutral facts (a weak station, a schedule); D happens after the visit and could reflect many moods, so it supports the inference far less directly."},
{id:"CS10", d:"CS", tier:"core",
 p:"Historian Priya Raghavan argues that the medieval mountain trade routes were contentious not because merchants quarreled constantly but because control of the passes was fiercely contested among rival city-states.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “contentious” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["Marked by heated competition", "Inclined to quarrel constantly", "Dependent on careful negotiation", "Unusually profitable"], a:0,
 e:"The text glosses the word directly: the routes were contentious because control of the passes was fiercely contested — contested, not merely quarrelsome. Choice B misreads the sentence, which denies constant quarreling; negotiation and profitability are never raised."},
{id:"CS11", d:"CS", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Because silverfish survive on crumbs and humidity alone, entomologist Marcus Feld has called them “living fossils of the domestic interior”: unchanged in form for roughly a hundred million years, they now make their living in the one habitat humans build without meaning to.",
 q:"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?",
 ch:["To question the reliability of Feld’s claims about insect evolution", "To explain how an ancient insect has remained unchanged while settling a human-made habitat", "To recommend lowering household humidity to control insect pests", "To compare silverfish with other insects that live in human homes"], a:1,
 e:"The text does two things at once: it dates the insect’s unchanged form and locates it in an accidental human habitat. The other choices invent a recommendation, a comparison, and a doubt that the text never expresses."},
{id:"CS12", d:"CS", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The museum’s new wing does not simply display textile art; it argues for it. Galleries are arranged to move visitors from functional weaving to abstract tapestry, and each wall text quietly makes the case that a craft long dismissed as domestic labor belongs beside painting and sculpture.",
 q:"Which choice best describes the structure of the text?",
 ch:["A question posed and then answered with visitor statistics", "A contrast between two rival curators’ philosophies", "A general claim followed by two specific features that illustrate it", "A chronological account of the museum’s acquisitions"], a:2,
 e:"The opening sentence states the claim (the wing argues for the art), and the remaining sentences supply the two features — the gallery arrangement and the wall texts — that carry it out. No chronology, statistics, or rival curators appear."},
{id:"CS13", d:"CS", tier:"elite",
 p:"<b>Text 1</b><br>Novelist Teodora Vlach defends the long sentence. A well-built long sentence, she says, holds its clauses in suspension the way a bridge holds its cables: the reader arrives at the period already knowing how the thought was assembled.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>Vlach’s engineering metaphor flatters the writer and burdens the reader. Most long sentences are not bridges but detours — the reader circles the same clause three times before being dropped, dizzy, at a destination a short sentence would have reached faster.",
 q:"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Vlach’s claim that a long sentence lets the reader see how a thought was assembled?",
 ch:["By insisting that assembly instructions belong in grammar textbooks rather than novels", "By noting that bridges, unlike sentences, are designed by teams", "By denying that readers ever notice sentence length at all", "By granting the craftsmanship while denying that most readers benefit enough to justify the extra effort"], a:3,
 e:"Text 2 does not dispute that long sentences can be well built — it calls the metaphor flattering, not false — but it rejects the payoff: circling clauses and arriving dizzy. It proposes no textbooks, discusses no design teams, and never claims readers are oblivious to length."},
{id:"CS14", d:"CS", tier:"core",
 p:"The committee’s endorsement was provisional: it would hold only until the funding report arrived, and every member understood that a single unfavorable line could dissolve it.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “provisional” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["Conditional", "Enthusiastic", "Unanimous", "Unwritten"], a:0,
 e:"The colon explains the term: the endorsement holds only under certain conditions, which is exactly what conditional means. Nothing in the text concerns enthusiasm, unanimity, or whether the endorsement was ever written down."},
{id:"CS15", d:"CS", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Botanist Ines Calvo spent two field seasons counting orchids in a single hectare of cloud forest — an exercise in patience that her graduate students nicknamed watching grass grow, except slower and wetter. The count mattered: her census revealed that three species flowered only after the year’s heaviest rains.",
 q:"Which choice best describes the function of the reference to the graduate students’ nickname?",
 ch:["It explains why orchids depend on heavy rainfall", "It concedes the tedium of the work before explaining why the work was worth doing", "It reveals that the graduate students opposed Calvo’s research", "It defines what a botanical census is"], a:1,
 e:"The nickname conveys how slow and damp the counting was; the pivot — “The count mattered” — then supplies the payoff. The students’ opposition, a definition, and the biology of rainfall never appear."},
{id:"CS16", d:"CS", tier:"elite",
 p:"Early reviews complained that the anthology skipped lightly over the 1930s. The editors’ response was to add not more pages but more silence: a plain gray leaf listing, in small type, the names of the presses that closed between 1929 and 1933.",
 q:"Which choice best describes how the anthology’s added section answers the reviewers’ complaint?",
 ch:["It disputes the reviewers’ dates for the press closures", "It adds new essays celebrating the presses of the 1930s", "It answers the charge of omission with a deliberately stark record of what was lost", "It apologizes for the gap and promises fuller coverage in a later edition"], a:2,
 e:"The gray leaf answers “skipped lightly over” by making the loss visible — a spare list of closed presses instead of a defense. No apology, no date dispute, and no celebratory essays are mentioned."},
{id:"CS17", d:"CS", tier:"core",
 p:"Linguist Hana Yusuf noticed that the teenagers she studied switched register mid-sentence: addressing a grandparent they borrowed the village elders’ slow, honor-laden phrasing, and turning back to a classmate they snapped instantly into slang.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “register” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["A written list of names", "The volume at which someone speaks", "A regional accent", "A style of speaking suited to a particular situation"], a:3,
 e:"The examples — honor-laden phrasing for elders, slang for classmates — show style shifting with audience, which is what register means here. A register is not a list, a loudness, or an accent."},
{id:"CS18", d:"CS", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Some nutrition writers claim that breakfast is metabolically special. Reviewing thirty controlled trials, however, Corinne Mbeki found that what looked like a breakfast effect was mostly a scheduling effect: people who ate early simply had more hours to be active afterward.",
 q:"Which choice best states how the text develops its point about breakfast?",
 ch:["It introduces a popular claim and then offers research that explains the claim differently", "It catalogs the foods that early eaters tend to prefer", "It shows that Mbeki’s thirty trials were too small to trust", "It concludes that everyone should skip breakfast"], a:0,
 e:"The text sets up the “metabolically special” claim and then replaces it with Mbeki’s scheduling explanation drawn from trials. No foods are cataloged, the trials are not impugned, and no advice about skipping breakfast is given."},
{id:"CS19", d:"CS", tier:"elite",
 p:"<b>Text 1</b><br>Astrophysicist Lena Brandt insists that the cold spots in the cosmic microwave background are not empty regions but regions whose light has simply not yet been assembled into stars — pockets of the universe still waiting to begin.<br><br><b>Text 2</b><br>Brandt’s “waiting rooms” make a lovely image, but the physics is stubborn: those spots are cold because the matter there is thin and old, not young, and no amount of poetic patience will turn them into star nurseries.",
 q:"Both texts suggest that the cold spots are",
 ch:["impossible to study with current instruments", "not what a casual description might make them seem", "on the verge of forming new stars", "poorer in matter than any other known region"], a:1,
 e:"Text 1 says the spots are misread as empty; Text 2 says the poetic image misstates their physics. Both converge on “not what they seem.” Only Text 1 hints at future stars, Text 2 compares the matter there only within the spots, and neither text mentions instruments."},
{id:"CS20", d:"CS", tier:"core",
 p:"City archivists were drowning in donation boxes: papers arrived faster than they could be cataloged. The fix was unglamorous — a standing rule that no box leaves the loading dock without a one-page inventory, signed by whoever accepted it.",
 q:"Which choice best describes the relationship between the two parts of the text?",
 ch:["The first part gives statistics; the second draws a moral lesson", "The first part asks a question; the second leaves it unanswered", "The first part presents a problem; the second presents the measure taken against it", "The first part praises the archivists; the second criticizes their methods"], a:2,
 e:"Overwhelmed staff are the problem; the standing inventory rule is the response. The other choices invent praise, statistics, lessons, and questions the text does not contain."},
{id:"CS21", d:"CS", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The new wetland park was designed to absorb the neighborhood’s storm runoff, but its planners also hoped it would absorb visitors — commuters on weekdays, families on weekends — until the city forgot the vacant field it replaced.",
 q:"As used in the text, what does the word “absorb” most nearly mean?",
 ch:["Reduce to nothingness", "Pay for entirely", "Learn thoroughly", "Take in or accommodate"], a:3,
 e:"The park takes in runoff and takes in people — accommodate. Nothing suggests annihilation, full payment, or learning."},
{id:"II10", d:"II", tier:"core",
 p:"In 1907, librarian Zhang Guiping built a book-exchange network using nothing but the ferries that crossed the river each morning. Readers left volumes in a crate at the dock; ferrymen ferried them; villages upriver and down returned them within the week. At its height the network moved about nine hundred volumes a month without a single requisition form.",
 q:"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?",
 ch:["A ferry-based book exchange ran efficiently through simple, self-enforcing routines", "Ferrymen preferred transporting books to transporting passengers", "Zhang Guiping invented the requisition form", "River villages in 1907 had no other access to books"], a:0,
 e:"The throughline is a low-overhead exchange that worked: crates, routine weekly returns, no paperwork. Ferry preferences, an invention of requisition forms, and “no other access” are all unsupported."},
{id:"II11", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The bakery’s ledgers show two price cuts for the same rye loaf in one winter, each followed within a fortnight by an increase in the flour order. The owner’s letters from the same months mention no new customers — only “the usual faces, more of them.”",
 q:"What can most reasonably be concluded about the price cuts?",
 ch:["They had no effect on the bakery’s flour purchasing", "They increased how much bread regular customers bought", "They drove the bakery’s regular customers away", "They were required by the city’s price controls"], a:1,
 e:"More of the usual faces plus larger flour orders after each cut means the regulars bought more. Abandonment contradicts the letters, price controls are never mentioned, and the flour orders refute “no effect.”"},
{id:"II12", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"Some historians credit the 1850s cholera outbreaks with forcing cities to build water systems; others say early pipe networks had begun decades before, driven by fire-fighting needs rather than disease.",
 q:"Which quotation from a city engineer’s memoir best supports the claim that fire-fighting, not cholera, drove the earliest pipe networks?",
 ch:["“We laid the first mains in 1848, six years before the outbreaks, and our reports from those years speak of hydrants, never of epidemics.”", "“After the sickness of 1854, the council could no longer refuse the water fund.”", "“Cholera changed every debate in this city, and ours was no exception.”", "“The physicians were the first to demand pipes beneath the streets.”"], a:0,
 e:"Only the first quotation places the mains before the outbreaks and ties them to hydrants. The second and third describe cholera’s influence, and the fourth credits physicians — the rival explanation."},
{id:"II13", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A study followed 1,200 households that switched from gas to induction cooktops. Average nitrogen dioxide levels in the kitchens fell by 42 percent; childhood asthma clinic visits among the households fell by 19 percent; energy bills fell by 6 percent.",
 q:"Which choice best describes what the study’s figures indicate?",
 ch:["Induction cooktops nearly eliminated childhood asthma", "Energy costs rose slightly after the switch", "The improvement in air quality was larger than either the health or the cost improvement", "Health outcomes improved without any measurable change in air quality"], a:2,
 e:"Forty-two percent (air) exceeds nineteen percent (health) and six percent (cost). A 42 percent fall contradicts “no measurable change”; 19 percent is far from “eliminated”; the bills fell rather than rose."},
{id:"II14", d:"II", tier:"core",
 p:"The viol built by Alvise Tiramo in 1689 was restored twice: once in 1882, when a cracked belly was replaced with pine, and again in 1974, when restorers removed the pine and reinstated a spruce belly carved from Tiramo’s original templates.",
 q:"According to the text, what happened during the 1974 restoration?",
 ch:["The viol was restored for the first time", "Tiramo’s original templates were destroyed", "A spruce belly was replaced with pine", "The pine belly installed in 1882 was replaced with spruce"], a:3,
 e:"The 1974 work removed the 1882 pine and reinstated spruce cut from the original templates. The other choices reverse the order of events, invent their destruction, or swap the two woods."},
{id:"II15", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"When the city repaved First Avenue, it moved every crosswalk thirty meters toward the corner with the bakery — a change too small for any map to record. Within a year the bakery had doubled its revenue; the pharmacy on the old corner cut its hours.",
 q:"What can most reasonably be concluded about the crosswalk change?",
 ch:["Pedestrian traffic shifted enough to matter for corner businesses", "The city intended the change to help the pharmacy", "The bakery had moved to a new location", "Maps failed to record the repaving entirely"], a:0,
 e:"A small shift in foot traffic produced a visible commercial reversal — the conclusion the figures support. Intent toward the pharmacy is not stated, the bakery never moved, and “too small for any map” concerns the crosswalks, not the whole repaving."},
{id:"II16", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Biologist Rosa Aoki claimed that the abandoned golf course became a functioning wetland within a decade — far faster than comparable sites.",
 q:"Which quotation from Aoki’s field report best supports the claim that the site’s transformation was unusually fast?",
 ch:["“Fifteen native amphibian species were breeding here by the eighth season; nearby conversions of similar size took twenty years to reach half that count.”", "“The clubhouse was demolished in the spring of the first year.”", "“Rainfall during the study period was close to the regional average.”", "“We surveyed the site on foot each month, in all weather.”"], a:0,
 e:"Only the first quotation compares the site’s timeline with similar sites, which is what “unusually fast” requires. The demolition, the rainfall, and the survey routine are facts without any comparison."},
{id:"II17", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The printing house run by Bartolomeo Serdo produced handbills for nearly every public execution in the city, yet its surviving ledgers record the trade soberly, line by line: “500 bills, gallows notice, ink and paper, 40 soldi.”",
 q:"Which choice best describes the function of the quoted ledger line in the text?",
 ch:["It suggests that the ledgers were forged", "It illustrates the flat, businesslike way a grim trade was recorded", "It proves that executions were popular entertainment", "It shows that Serdo undercharged for his work"], a:1,
 e:"The line’s plain accounting tone is the point — the trade recorded “soberly.” Nothing is claimed about attendance, pricing, or forgery."},
{id:"II18", d:"II", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Passenger pigeons vanished in 1914; the great auk decades earlier. What the two extinctions shared was not habitat loss but a market: both birds were harvested commercially at industrial scale, and both were, until near the end, extraordinarily abundant — an abundance that invited the assumption that no harvest could exhaust them.",
 q:"Based on the text, the author suggests that understanding these extinctions requires",
 ch:["proving that the last captive bird died in 1914", "studying the migration routes of both birds", "recognizing that abundance did not protect against commercial overharvest", "accepting that habitat loss was the primary cause in both cases"], a:2,
 e:"The text names the shared market logic and the false comfort of abundance. Habitat loss is explicitly ruled out as the common factor; the 1914 date and migration routes are not the point being made."},
{id:"II19", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"The city offered two community-garden designs: Riverside rows and Hillside squares. Gardeners chose freely. Of 100 new gardeners, 60 chose Riverside and 40 chose Hillside. A year later, of the 50 gardeners who quit, 35 had held Hillside plots and 15 had held Riverside plots.",
 q:"Which choice best reflects what the figures show?",
 ch:["The two designs proved equally popular with newcomers", "Riverside plots were abandoned at a higher rate than Hillside plots", "Most gardeners who quit held Riverside plots", "Newcomers favored Riverside, and Hillside plots were abandoned at a far higher rate"], a:3,
 e:"Sixty percent of newcomers picked Riverside, and abandonment ran 35 of 40 (87.5 percent) at Hillside versus 15 of 60 (25 percent) at Riverside. Equal popularity, a Riverside abandonment edge, and a Riverside majority of leavers all contradict the numbers."},
{id:"II20", d:"II", tier:"core",
 p:"The library’s only rule about the reading room’s green chairs was that no one could reserve them. Regulars arrived at opening anyway; one regular, a retired ferry pilot, kept a folded newspaper on the same chair’s armrest for eleven years.",
 q:"What can most reasonably be concluded about the green chairs?",
 ch:["Regulars treated particular chairs as theirs despite the no-reservation rule", "The library fined visitors who occupied chairs too long", "The ferry pilot was the library’s oldest visitor", "The chairs were replaced after eleven years"], a:0,
 e:"A newspaper parked on the same armrest for a decade shows quiet possession against the rule’s spirit. Fines, seniority, and replacement are never stated."},
{id:"II21", d:"II", tier:"elite",
 p:"Economist Fenna Dijkstra attributes the sudden 1680s collapse in tulip futures prices to a new exchange rule that required sellers to deliver actual bulbs rather than paper promises. The rule, she notes, took effect only weeks before prices fell.",
 q:"Which finding, if true, would most weaken Dijkstra’s attribution?",
 ch:["Some traders ignored the delivery rule and were fined", "Tulip futures prices fell on the same schedule in cities where no delivery rule existed", "Prices had already drifted downward for two winters before the rule took effect", "Bulb growers supported the new rule enthusiastically"], a:1,
 e:"A simultaneous fall where the rule never existed breaks the causal link the attribution depends on. A pre-existing drift would complicate the story but not break it as directly; growers’ support and traders’ fines say nothing about whether the rule caused the collapse."},
];
/* ================= QUESTION BANK — R&W part 2: SEC + EOI ================= */
window.RW2 = [
/* ---------- Standard English Conventions ---------- */
{id:"SEC01", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The archive’s most curious holding <span class='ul'>are</span> a single album of double exposures, each of which merges two portraits of the same sitter taken decades apart.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["are","is","were being","have been"], a:1,
 e:"The subject is the singular “holding,” not the nearer plural “exposures”: one album is the holding, so it “is.” Options A, C, and D all agree with the wrong noun or add aspect the sentence does not need — a classic agreement trap built on the phrase “of double exposures.”"},
{id:"SEC02", d:"SEC", tier:"elite",
 p:"A leatherback turtle lays roughly eighty eggs per clutch, yet fewer than one hatchling in a thousand survives to adulthood — a statistic that explains why volunteers guard <span class='ul'>their</span> nests so fiercely through the hatching season.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["their","the turtles’","its","his or her"], a:1,
 e:"“Their” is ambiguous: the sentence’s plurals (eggs, volunteers) leave the reader unsure whose nests are meant, and the logic clearly points to the turtles. “The turtles’” removes the ambiguity. “Its” mismatches a plural noun, and “his or her” introduces an unnecessary singular construction."},
{id:"SEC03", d:"SEC", tier:"elite",
 p:"Lighthouse keepers once recorded wind speed and visibility every hour<span class='ul'>,</span> the resulting ledgers now give climatologists a century of uninterrupted coastal weather data.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["hour, the resulting ledgers","hour; the resulting ledgers","hour, and the resulting ledgers now gives","hour, consequently the resulting ledgers"], a:1,
 e:"“…every hour, the resulting ledgers…” joins two independent clauses with only a comma — a splice. A semicolon (option B) joins them correctly. Option C fixes the splice but adds an agreement error (“ledgers now gives”), and option D’s comma before “consequently” is still a splice — conjunctive adverbs cannot be attached by commas alone."},
{id:"SEC04", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The regional science fair awarded medals to projects submitted by several students. Judges singled out the <span class='ul'>student's</span> experimental designs for unusually careful controls.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["student's","students’","students project's","student’s projects’"], a:1,
 e:"The designs belong to the several students mentioned in the previous sentence, so the possessive must be plural: “students’.” Option A makes it singular, C shifts the possessive onto “project,” and D attaches two possessive marks to different nouns, producing an ungrammatical pile-up."},
{id:"SEC05", d:"SEC", tier:"elite",
 p:"Having surveyed the reef for over a decade, <span class='ul'>the coral bleaching patterns were mapped by Dr. Osei’s team</span> with unprecedented precision.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["the coral bleaching patterns were mapped by Dr. Osei’s team with unprecedented precision","Dr. Osei’s team mapped the coral bleaching patterns with unprecedented precision","the coral bleaching patterns, mapped with unprecedented precision by Dr. Osei’s team","with unprecedented precision the bleaching patterns were mapped by Dr. Osei’s team"], a:1,
 e:"The opening modifier (“Having surveyed…”) must describe the subject that follows — the surveying was done by the team, not by “patterns.” Option B supplies the team as subject. Option A keeps the dangler; C creates a fragment with no main verb; D is still dangling and wordier to boot."},
{id:"SEC06", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"When the railroad finally reached the valley in 1888, farmers <span class='ul'>have shipped</span> their fruit east within hours of picking for the very first time.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["have shipped","shipped","are shipping","will ship"], a:1,
 e:"The time anchor “in 1888” is a finished moment in the past, so simple past “shipped” is correct. The present perfect “have shipped” cannot attach to a completed past date; “are shipping” and “will ship” relocate the action to the present and future, contradicting the sentence."},
{id:"SEC07", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The museum’s renovation plan calls for <span class='ul'>restoring the fresco cycle, to rewire the gallery lighting, and cataloguing</span> the manuscript collection.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["restoring the fresco cycle, to rewire the gallery lighting, and cataloguing","restoring the fresco cycle, rewiring the gallery lighting, and cataloguing","to restore the fresco cycle, rewiring the gallery lighting, and to catalogue","restore the fresco cycle, rewiring the gallery lighting, and catalogue"], a:1,
 e:"Items in a series must be grammatically parallel: all three gerunds — restoring, rewiring, cataloguing. Option B achieves that. A and C mix infinitives with gerunds, and D shifts into bare verbs, breaking the parallelism required after “calls for.”"},
{id:"SEC08", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Each of the exhibit’s interactive stations <span class='ul'>demonstrate</span> a different principle of optics, from refraction to polarization.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["demonstrate","demonstrates","have demonstrated","were demonstrating"], a:1,
 e:"“Each of the… stations” is grammatically singular — the verb must agree with “each,” not with “stations.” Hence “demonstrates.” The other options agree with the plural noun or add tense/aspect the sentence neither needs nor supports."},
{id:"SEC09", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The audition requires three things<span class='ul'>;</span> a prepared monologue, two contrasting songs, and sheet music in the singer’s key.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["things; a prepared monologue","things: a prepared monologue","things, a prepared monologue","things of a prepared monologue"], a:1,
 e:"A colon is required when what follows is a list that explains or exemplifies what precedes it — exactly this case. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, a bare comma produces a misreading, and “things of” is ungrammatical."},
{id:"SEC10", d:"SEC", tier:"elite",
 p:"Enrollment in the county’s engineering programs <span class='ul'>has doubled the rate from a decade ago</span>, a shift the community college attributes to new dual-credit courses.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["has doubled the rate from a decade ago","is double the rate from a decade ago","has doubled more than a decade ago","doubled a decade ago’s rate more"], a:1,
 e:"An enrollment figure cannot “double a rate” — only “be double the rate” is a logical comparison of two numbers. Option B fixes the illogical comparison. C drops the comparison entirely (“doubled more than a decade ago” compares nothing), and D is garbled syntax."},
{id:"SEC11", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The expedition had packed for a mild autumn<span class='ul'>,</span> however, the team pressed on toward the ice shelf after the first blizzard warning.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["autumn, however, the team","autumn; however, the team","autumn, however; the team","autumn, however the team"], a:1,
 e:"“However” is an adverb, not a conjunction: it cannot be glued to the next clause with a comma. A semicolon before “however” and a comma after it (option B) correctly joins the two independent clauses. C and D misplace the punctuation; A is the original splice."},
{id:"SEC12", d:"SEC", tier:"elite",
 p:"The fellowship went to the only applicant <span class='ul'>which</span> had completed fieldwork on three continents before graduating.",
 q:"Which choice best states the underlined portion?",
 ch:["which","who","whom","whose"], a:1,
 e:"The relative pronoun refers to “applicant,” a person, and is the subject of “had completed” — so “who.” “Which” is reserved for things, “whom” would require an object role, and “whose” signals possession rather than agency."},

/* ---------- Expression of Ideas ---------- */
{id:"EOI01", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"The hotel’s dessert menu still lists thirty items, a relic of its golden era. <span class='ul'>Consequently</span>, the kitchen has been streamlined until it produces a single celebratory plate.",
 q:"The writer wants a transition that shows the contrast between the menu and the kitchen. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?",
 ch:["Consequently","By contrast","Similarly","In addition"], a:1,
 e:"The two sentences describe opposite states — a thirty-item menu versus a one-plate kitchen — so the logical relationship is contrast: “By contrast.” “Consequently” claims a false cause-effect, while “Similarly” and “In addition” assert sameness or addition, the exact reverse of the relationship."},
{id:"EOI02", d:"EOI", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Wildfire smoke carries particles fine enough to cross from the lungs into the bloodstream. <span class='ul'>For instance</span>, public health agencies now issue air-quality warnings even when the nearest flames are hundreds of miles away.",
 q:"Which choice best states the function of the underlined transition?",
 ch:["It signals a cause-and-effect relationship between the two sentences","It signals that the second sentence provides an example — but the second sentence is a consequence, not an example","It signals a contrast between the two sentences","It signals that the second sentence restates the first"], a:1,
 e:"The agency warnings follow from the fine-particle fact; the relationship is cause and effect, so “For this reason” is the fitting transition. “For instance” would promise an example of smoke or particles, and neither a contrast nor a restatement exists between the sentences."},
{id:"EOI03", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"The novel’s fragmentary structure demands patience: whole chapters arrive as lists, letters, and unanswered messages. <span class='ul'>Therefore</span>, that very structure is what lets the book hold three timelines inside a single frame, which is why few editors have urged the author to abandon it.",
 q:"The writer wants a transition that signals a concession — acknowledging the difficulty before defending the structure. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?",
 ch:["Therefore","Even so","Meanwhile","For example"], a:1,
 e:"“Even so” grants the first sentence’s point (the structure is demanding) and then pivots against it — the concession the writer wants. “Therefore” wrongly asserts that the difficulty causes the defense; “Meanwhile” adds simultaneity; “For example” promises an illustration."},
{id:"EOI04", d:"EOI", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The restored meadow supports an unusually dense pollinator population. <span class='ul'>As a result</span>, a single morning’s survey last June logged forty-one bee species within a hundred-meter radius.",
 q:"Which choice best states the function of the underlined transition?",
 ch:["It signals that the second sentence provides supporting evidence — an example, not a result","It signals a cause-and-effect relationship","It signals a contrast between the sentences","It signals that the second sentence concludes the passage"], a:0,
 e:"The forty-one-species survey is a concrete instance backing the “dense population” claim — an example, not an outcome produced by it. So “For instance” fits, while “As a result” manufactures a causal link the data do not claim. No contrast or conclusion appears."},
{id:"EOI05", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Cheval Bluff wetland was drained for farmland in 1912.<br>• A community-led project re-flooded 240 hectares of the site in 2019.<br>• Within three years, endangered striped frogs were breeding at the site again.<br>• The project was funded by a $1.4 million local levy.<br><br>The student wants to emphasize <b>how quickly the wetland’s ecology recovered</b>. Which choice most effectively uses information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 q:"Which choice most effectively uses information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 ch:["The Cheval Bluff wetland, drained for farmland in 1912, was re-flooded by a community-led project in 2019.","Just three years after volunteers re-flooded 240 hectares of the drained Cheval Bluff wetland, endangered striped frogs were breeding there again.","The Cheval Bluff re-flooding project was funded by a $1.4 million levy approved by local voters.","After the wetland was drained in 1912, striped frogs vanished from the entire region until 2019."], a:1,
 e:"Only option B joins the recovery to a short clock — “just three years” — which is the emphasis the student wants. A and C are accurate but neutral (dates and funding), and D fabricates a detail: the notes never say frogs vanished “from the entire region,” a classic overreach trap in synthesis items."},
{id:"EOI06", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"While researching a mathematician, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Maria Andrade published her first paper at nineteen.<br>• Her 1961 theorem unified two branches of number theory.<br>• She declined every endowed chair she was offered.<br>• She spent four decades teaching at a public evening school.<br>• A crater on Mercury is named after her.<br><br>The student wants to emphasize <b>Andrade’s preference for teaching over prestige</b>. Which choice most effectively uses information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 q:"Which choice most effectively uses information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 ch:["Andrade, whose 1961 theorem unified two branches of number theory, declined every endowed chair she was offered and instead spent four decades teaching at a public evening school.","A crater on Mercury bears Andrade’s name, one of the highest honors in science.","Andrade published her first mathematical paper at the unusually young age of nineteen.","Andrade’s 1961 theorem remains central to number theory courses today."], a:0,
 e:"Option A sets the prestige she refused (endowed chairs) directly against the teaching she chose — the exact contrast requested. B and C emphasize honor and precocity instead, and D fabricates a claim absent from the notes (“remains central today”), a detail the student cannot assert from the source."},
{id:"EOI07", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"While researching a space mission, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Hyakutake Mapper spent eleven years orbiting comet 103P.<br>• Its drill returned fourteen sealed ice samples to Earth.<br>• In 2030 a dust strike severed its antenna, ending all communication.<br>• Total mission cost: $890 million.<br><br>The student wants to emphasize <b>that a successful mission ended unexpectedly</b>. Which choice most effectively uses information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 q:"Which choice most effectively uses information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 ch:["After eleven successful years and fourteen sealed ice samples delivered, the Hyakutake Mapper fell silent in 2030 when a single dust strike severed its antenna.","The Hyakutake Mapper mission cost $890 million from launch to the end of communication.","The Hyakutake Mapper was designed to orbit comet 103P and to drill sealed samples from surface ice.","A single dust particle travels at roughly 60 kilometers per second at comet orbit distances."], a:0,
 e:"Option A names the achievements first (“eleven successful years,” “fourteen samples”) and then delivers the abrupt, accidental ending — the “successful mission, unexpected end” arc. B emphasizes cost, C mere design goals, and D is not even drawn from the notes, a common synthesis distractor."},
{id:"EOI08", d:"EOI", tier:"advanced",
 p:"While researching language preservation, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Only twelve fluent speakers of Wailimu remained as of 2020.<br>• The community school began Wailimu immersion classes that year.<br>• High-school students now record elders’ stories every week.<br>• The class’s digital archive passed 4,000 entries in 2024.<br><br>The student wants to emphasize <b>the role young people are playing in the language’s survival</b>. Which choice most effectively uses information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 q:"Which choice most effectively uses information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 ch:["As of 2020, only twelve fluent speakers of Wailimu remained.","In Wailimu immersion classrooms, high-school students sustain the language by recording elders’ stories weekly for a digital archive that passed 4,000 entries in 2024.","The community school’s immersion program began in 2020.","The digital archive is stored on servers maintained by the regional university."], a:1,
 e:"Option B puts the students at the center of the sentence doing the sustaining work — the requested emphasis. A dwells on the bleak count, C records only a start date, and D invents a detail no note supports (university servers), which also makes it unusable in a synthesis answer."},
{id:"SEC13", d:"SEC", tier:"core",
 p:"The observatory’s public nights drew hundreds of <span class='ul'>stargazers, the</span> volunteers rarely had enough telescopes to go around.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["stargazers the", "stargazers, whom the", "stargazers; the", "stargazers, the"], a:2,
 e:"Two independent clauses cannot be joined by a bare comma or by nothing at all, but a semicolon joins them cleanly. Option D repeats the comma splice; Option A fuses the clauses; Option B turns the second clause into a fragment with “whom.”"},
{id:"SEC14", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The gallery’s collection of miniature portraits, each painted on ivory no larger than a playing card, <span class='ul'>remain</span> among the museum’s most fragile holdings.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["have remained", "are remaining", "remain", "remains"], a:3,
 e:"The subject is the singular “collection,” not the nearer plural “portraits”: one collection remains among the holdings. Option C agrees with the wrong noun; Options A and B add aspect or agreement errors the sentence cannot support."},
{id:"SEC15", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"When a honeybee finds a rich patch of flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a dance that tells <span class='ul'>their</span> hive mates the patch’s direction and distance.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["its", "it’s", "his or her", "their"], a:0,
 e:"The antecedent is the singular “a honeybee,” and the hive mates belong to it: “its.” Option D agrees with a plural that does not exist; Option B is the contraction of “it is”; Option C reaches for a gendered pair nothing in the sentence calls for."},
{id:"SEC16", d:"SEC", tier:"core",
 p:"Long before radar made instant tracking possible, ornithologists <span class='ul'>have tracked</span> the paths of migrating hawks by standing on ridges and simply watching, hour after hour.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["have tracked", "tracked", "will track", "are tracking"], a:1,
 e:"“Long before radar made…” fixes the era in the past, so the simple past “tracked” fits. Option A wrongly links the action to the present; Options C and D are impossible for the period described."},
{id:"SEC17", d:"SEC", tier:"core",
 p:"The vineyard’s oldest block still bears the <span class='ul'>founders</span> signature on its deed: a shaky “B” for Basilio Ferro, who planted it in 1921.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["founders is", "founders", "founder’s", "founders’"], a:2,
 e:"One founder planted the block — Basilio Ferro — so the deed bears “the founder’s signature.” Option B drops the possessive; Option D would require plural founders; Option A is not a possessive form at all."},
{id:"SEC18", d:"SEC", tier:"elite",
 p:"The canal locks were rebuilt in 1908<span class='ul'>, however,</span> the first cargo barges did not cross the summit until 1912.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:[", however,", "; however", ", however", "; however,"], a:3,
 e:"“However” is a conjunctive adverb, not a conjunction: joining two independent clauses with it takes a semicolon before it and a comma after. Option A is the comma splice already in the text; Options B and C each drop the comma that must follow “however.”"},
{id:"SEC19", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Standing nearly nine meters tall and cast from melted-down cannon, <span class='ul'>it is easy</span> for visitors to mistake the statue for a memorial to a general.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["the statue is easy", "easy", "being easy", "it is easy"], a:0,
 e:"The opening description must attach to the thing it describes: “the statue is easy for visitors to mistake…” Option D leaves “it” pointing at nothing — the classic dangling modifier; Options B and C leave the sentence without a main clause."},
{id:"SEC20", d:"SEC", tier:"core",
 p:"The lighthouse keeper’s log records every storm without complaint, though it also betrays <span class='ul'>it’s</span> author’s growing respect for the sea.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["it’s", "its", "it’s’", "it is"], a:1,
 e:"The possessive of “it” is “its” — no apostrophe. Option A reads “it is author’s”; Option C is not an English form; Option D cannot own anything."},
{id:"SEC21", d:"SEC", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The field guide advises beginners to learn ten birds well rather than to skim the accounts of a hundred, to visit one habitat in every season, and <span class='ul'>keeping</span> a notebook that names what surprised them.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["by keeping", "keeping", "to keep", "keep"], a:2,
 e:"The series runs “to learn…, to visit…,” so the third item must also be an infinitive: “to keep.” Options B, D, and A break the parallel structure with a gerund, a bare verb, and a prepositional phrase."},
{id:"SEC22", d:"SEC", tier:"core",
 p:"The town council voted to restore the bandstand rather than <span class='ul'>demolishing</span> it and build a larger stage elsewhere.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["to demolish", "demolished", "demolishing", "demolish"], a:3,
 e:"After “voted to restore… rather than,” the base form pairs with the earlier infinitive: restore rather than demolish. Options C, A, and B each break the parallel structure in a different way."},
{id:"SEC23", d:"SEC", tier:"elite",
 p:"There <span class='ul'>was</span>, by the mayor’s own accounting, at least three unreported flooding events along the new seawall last spring.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["were", "are", "has been", "was"], a:0,
 e:"In a “there” sentence the real subject follows the verb: “three unreported flooding events,” plural, so “were.” Option D agrees with the placeholder “there” instead; Option B clashes with “last spring”; Option C is both singular and wrong in tense."},
{id:"SEC24", d:"SEC", tier:"core",
 p:"The print shop’s proudest artifact <span class='ul'>being</span> a hand press that survived three moves and one fire.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?",
 ch:["being", "is", "was being", "has been"], a:1,
 e:"“Being” leaves the sentence without a finite verb — a fragment. “Is” supplies the missing main verb; Options C and D add aspect or timing the sentence does not call for."},
{id:"EOI09", d:"EOI", tier:"core",
 p:"The desert’s springs rarely freeze <span class='ul'>although</span> the salt content lowers their freezing point well below what any local winter can manage.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?",
 ch:["likewise", "although", "because", "moreover"], a:2,
 e:"The second clause explains why the springs resist freezing — a causal relation, so “because.” “Although” asserts a contrast that does not exist; “moreover” and “likewise” add or compare rather than explain."},
{id:"EOI10", d:"EOI", tier:"advanced",
 p:"By March, the archive had cataloged its ten-thousandth oral history. <span class='ul'>For instance</span>, the staff did not celebrate; they were already drafting the funding appeal the next ten thousand would require.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?",
 ch:["In other words", "Similarly", "For instance", "Even so"], a:3,
 e:"“Even so” concedes the milestone and pivots to the contrast — no celebration, straight to work. “For instance” claims an example of the milestone; “In other words” would restate it; “Similarly” asserts a parallel. None fits the pivot."},
{id:"EOI11", d:"EOI", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The village council wanted to show that its new night bus served more than commuters.",
 q:"Which choice provides the most relevant evidence for this purpose?",
 ch:["Ridership logs showed that nearly half of the boardings between 10 p.m. and 1 a.m. were nurses, kitchen staff, and cleaners finishing shifts", "The bus was painted the same blue as the council’s other vehicles", "Commuters reported that they liked the new bus", "The council met on Thursdays to review the routes"], a:0,
 e:"Only the ridership breakdown, with its night-shift occupations, shows use beyond commuters. Paint color, vague approval, and meeting schedules support nothing about who rides at night."},
{id:"EOI12", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"[1] The Bedlington terrier looks nothing like a typical terrier: it is lamb-like, curly, and mild-eyed. [2] Beneath the wool, however, is a working dog bred to hunt rats in the mines of northern England. [3] Early show judges were routinely fooled by the appearance. [4] The breed remains rare outside Britain to this day.",
 q:"The writer wants to add this sentence: “Competitions soon began pairing the terriers against live rats to settle the matter.” Where does it best fit?",
 ch:["After sentence [2]", "After sentence [3]", "After sentence [4]", "Before sentence [1]"], a:1,
 e:"“To settle the matter” refers to the judges being fooled in sentence [3], so the added sentence belongs immediately after it. Before [2] there is no matter to settle; after [4] it derails the closing thought."},
{id:"EOI13", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• The Auralux Festival began in 1998 as a single-evening concert in a converted grain silo.<br>• By 2019 it ran for nine days across four outdoor stages.<br>• Organizers credit the silo’s unusual echo with the festival’s early reputation.<br>• Attendance in 2019 exceeded 60,000.",
 q:"The student wants to present the festival’s growth to a general audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 ch:["In 2019 the Auralux Festival had over 60,000 attendees, nine days, four stages, and a silo", "A grain silo with an unusual echo started a festival", "The Auralux Festival grew from a single evening in a grain silo in 1998 to nine days on four stages drawing more than 60,000 people by 2019", "The Auralux Festival is an outdoor event known for an unusual echo"], a:2,
 e:"Choice C states a clear beginning and end point — the growth arc a general audience needs. Choices D and B list fragments without the growth; Choice A strings note phrases together rather than presenting them."},
{id:"EOI14", d:"EOI", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The reviewer praised the novel’s dialogue but called its plotting <span class='ul'>suspenseful</span>: three separate chapters each ended with a character conveniently overhearing the exact conversation needed to move the story along.",
 q:"Which choice best replaces the underlined word to make the description accurate?",
 ch:["lyrical", "economical", "suspenseful", "predictable"], a:3,
 e:"Characters repeatedly overhearing exactly what the plot needs makes outcomes easy to foresee: “predictable.” “Suspenseful” is contradicted by the colon’s evidence; “lyrical” fits dialogue, not plotting; “economical” is the opposite of a repeated crutch."},
{id:"EOI15", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"The geyser’s eruptions were famously punctual for decades — within a ten-minute window, year after year. <span class='ul'>Likewise</span>, after the 1983 earthquake rearranged its plumbing, predictions became ranges, not appointments.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?",
 ch:["Then", "For example", "In short", "Likewise"], a:0,
 e:"The second sentence marks a chronological turn: “Then.” “Likewise” falsely asserts sameness; “For example” misreads the earthquake as an instance of punctuality; “In short” would summarize rather than advance the account."},
{id:"EOI16", d:"EOI", tier:"core",
 p:"Claim: The ferry terminal’s renovation succeeded because the planners asked the people who used it daily.",
 q:"Which choice provides the most relevant evidence for this claim?",
 ch:["Planners held one public meeting, which few people attended", "Design changes — wider ramps, a sheltered queue, outlets for charging — each came from ferry workers’ and commuters’ suggestions", "The renovation finished two weeks ahead of schedule", "The terminal’s murals were painted by a local artist"], a:1,
 e:"The first sentence ties specific features to daily users’ input — the claim’s mechanism. The schedule, the murals, and a sparsely attended meeting do not show user-driven success."},
{id:"EOI17", d:"EOI", tier:"elite",
 p:"[1] The Voynich manuscript’s plants match no known species. [2] Its script matches no known language. [3] Carbon dating places its parchment in the early fifteenth century. [4] Every decipherment claim since has collapsed under review.",
 q:"The writer wants to add this sentence: “That much, at least, everyone agrees on.” Where does it best fit?",
 ch:["Before sentence [1]", "After sentence [2]", "After sentence [3]", "After sentence [4]"], a:2,
 e:"The added sentence concedes a point of agreement — the carbon date is the one uncontested fact — so it follows sentence [3]. The plant, script, and decipherment claims are all disputed, and before [1] there is nothing yet to concede."},
{id:"EOI18", d:"EOI", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Colonial surveyors mapped the coastline with chains and sextants, accepting errors that today would end a career. <span class='ul'>Nevertheless</span>, the same charts remained in use for a century, their errors faithfully copied by each new cartographer.",
 q:"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?",
 ch:["Meanwhile", "In contrast", "Nevertheless", "Consequently"], a:3,
 e:"The copying of errors is a result of the surveyors’ method — a causal link, so “Consequently.” “Nevertheless” promises a contrast the text does not deliver; “meanwhile” and “in contrast” misrelate the two sentences."},
{id:"EOI19", d:"EOI", tier:"advanced",
 p:"While researching sandhill cranes, a student has taken the following notes:<br>• Roughly 80 percent of the world’s sandhill cranes stop at Nebraska’s Platte River each spring.<br>• They gather there to build up fat reserves before continuing their migration.<br>• Crane-watching visits bring millions of dollars to local businesses each March.",
 q:"The student wants to explain why the Platte River stopover matters. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?",
 ch:["Most of the world’s sandhill cranes refuel on the Platte each spring, and the gathering has become economically vital to the region", "Sandhill cranes migrate in spring and in fall", "The Platte River is in Nebraska, and cranes are large birds", "Local economies receive millions of dollars each March"], a:0,
 e:"Choice A connects the two reasons the stopover matters — the biology and the economy. The other choices are fragments of the notes that carry neither reason, let alone the link between them."},
{id:"EOI20", d:"EOI", tier:"core",
 p:"The report described the town’s flood defenses as <span class='ul'>state-of-the-art</span>: the levees had not been raised since 1978, and the pump station ran on parts scavenged from a demolished school.",
 q:"Which choice best replaces the underlined phrase to make the description accurate?",
 ch:["state-of-the-art", "neglected", "controversial", "experimental"], a:1,
 e:"Unraised levees and scavenged pump parts show neglect: “neglected.” “State-of-the-art” is contradicted by the colon’s evidence; neither controversy nor experimentation is described anywhere."},
];
/* ================= QUESTION BANK — Math (static items) =================
   Every numeric answer was computed and re-checked. Notation uses <sup>/<sub> tags. */
window.MATH = [
/* ---------- Algebra ---------- */
{id:"ALG01", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"A mobile plan charges $12 per month plus $0.05 for each megabyte of data over the 5 GB allowance. A rival plan charges a flat $22 per month with no overage fee. A customer uses the same number of megabytes over the allowance every month.",
 q:"For how many megabytes of overage data per month do the two plans cost the same?",
 ch:["800","200","440","120"], a:1,
 e:"Set 12 + 0.05x = 22. Then 0.05x = 10, so x = 10 ÷ 0.05 = 200 MB. Choice A (800) forgets to divide by the per-MB rate; 440 and 120 come from fumbling the subtraction (22 − 12 = 10)."},
{id:"ALG02", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Line ℓ passes through the points (2, 5) and (6, 13).",
 q:"Which equation is the equation of line ℓ?",
 ch:["y = 2x + 1","y = 2x + 3","y = ½x + 4","y = 8x − 11"], a:0,
 e:"Slope = (13 − 5)/(6 − 2) = 8/4 = 2. Then y − 5 = 2(x − 2) gives y = 2x + 1. Choice B uses the right slope but plugs a point in wrongly; C halves the slope; D treats slope as if it were 8."},
{id:"ALG03", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"A delivery driver earns $96 for each full day of work plus $0.55 for each package delivered. On a given day, the driver wants to earn at least $190.",
 q:"Which inequality represents this situation, where p is the number of packages delivered that day?",
 ch:["0.55p + 96 ≥ 190","0.55p + 96 ≤ 190","96p + 0.55 ≥ 190","0.55(p + 96) ≥ 190"], a:0,
 e:"Daily pay = 96 (flat) + 0.55 per package, and “at least $190” means ≥ 190: 0.55p + 96 ≥ 190. Choice C swaps the roles of the flat fee and the per-package rate, and D distributes the per-package rate across the day fee — a structure that has no meaning here."},
{id:"ALG04", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The system below has solution (x, y):<br>2x + y = 11<br>x − y = 4",
 q:"What is the value of x?",
 ch:["3","5","6","15/2"], a:1,
 e:"Add the equations: (2x + y) + (x − y) = 3x, and 11 + 4 = 15, so 3x = 15 and x = 5 (with y = 1). The elimination works precisely because y’s coefficients are +1 and −1."},
{id:"ALG05", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"The total cost of a home renovation, in dollars, is modeled by C(t) = 2,500 + 65t, where t is the number of days after work begins.",
 q:"What does the number 65 represent in this model?",
 ch:["The cost that is added each additional day of work","The cost of the renovation on the day work begins","The total number of days the renovation takes","The cost added for every 65 days of work"], a:0,
 e:"In a linear model y = b + mx, the coefficient m is the per-unit rate: each extra day adds $65. The 2,500 is the starting cost, so B describes the wrong constant; C and D confuse the rate with a quantity of days."},
{id:"ALG06", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A climbing gym charges a one-time membership fee of $75 plus $8 for each class attended.",
 q:"Which equation gives C, the total cost in dollars for a member who attends c classes?",
 ch:["C = 8c + 75","C = 75c + 8","C = 8(c + 75)","C = 83c"], a:0,
 e:"The variable quantity (classes) multiplies the per-class rate: 8c; the one-time fee is added: + 75. So C = 8c + 75. Choice B swaps fee and rate, C wrongly multiplies the fee by classes, and D folds the fee into the per-class rate."},
{id:"ALG07", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"An artisan sells each ceramic bowl for $18. Materials cost $7 per bowl, and renting the studio kiln costs a flat $110 per week.",
 q:"What is the minimum number of bowls the artisan must sell in a week for revenue to exceed costs?",
 ch:["10","11","12","6"], a:1,
 e:"Revenue 18b must exceed cost 7b + 110: 18b > 7b + 110 → 11b > 110 → b > 10. The smallest whole number greater than 10 is 11. Choice A (exactly 10) gives revenue equal to cost, not exceeding it — read the strict inequality."},
{id:"ALG08", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Water drains from a tank at a constant rate. The function f(t) = 480 − 12t gives the number of liters in the tank t minutes after draining begins.",
 q:"After how many minutes will the tank be empty?",
 ch:["12","40","480","468"], a:1,
 e:"Empty means f(t) = 0: 480 − 12t = 0 → t = 480/12 = 40 minutes. Choice A confuses the drain rate with the time; D simply subtracts the rate once."},
{id:"ALG09", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"The system below has solution (x, y):<br>4x − 3y = 10<br>2x + 3y = 8",
 q:"What is the value of x?",
 ch:["3","6","9/2","2"], a:0,
 e:"The y-terms are +3y and −3y, so adding eliminates y: 6x = 18, x = 3. (Then y = 2/3.) Choice B solves 6x incorrectly; C and D come from subtracting or misaligning terms instead of adding."},
{id:"ALG10", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Two storage companies charge monthly fees. Company A charges $150 plus $20 per month of use; Company B charges $90 plus $35 per month of use.",
 q:"After how many months of use do the two companies charge the same total amount?",
 ch:["4","6","10","15"], a:0,
 e:"Set 150 + 20m = 90 + 35m. Then 60 = 15m, so m = 4. Before month 4, B is cheaper; after it, A is — checking both sides at m = 4 gives $230 each, confirming the answer."},

/* ---------- Advanced Math ---------- */
{id:"ADV01", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"A model rocket’s height above the ground, in meters, is modeled by h(t) = −5t<sup>2</sup> + 20t + 1, where t is the time in seconds after launch.",
 q:"What is the maximum height the rocket reaches?",
 ch:["20 meters","21 meters","41 meters","2 meters"], a:1,
 e:"Maximum occurs at t = −b/(2a) = −20/(2·(−5)) = 2 seconds. Then h(2) = −5(4) + 40 + 1 = 21 meters. Choice C (41) adds the coefficients without evaluating; A answers “time-like” thinking about b; D reports the time of the maximum, not its height."},
{id:"ADV02", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"x<sup>2</sup> − 6x − 16",
 q:"Which expression is equivalent to the one shown above?",
 ch:["(x − 8)(x + 2)","(x − 4)(x − 4)","(x + 8)(x − 2)","(x − 16)(x + 1)"], a:0,
 e:"You need two numbers multiplying to −16 and summing to −6: −8 and +2. Check: (x − 8)(x + 2) = x<sup>2</sup> − 6x − 16. The other pairs give −8x + 16, +6x − 16, and −15x − 16 respectively."},
{id:"ADV03", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"A bacterial culture starts with 500 cells and doubles in size every 3 hours.",
 q:"Which function gives N, the number of cells, t hours after the culture is started?",
 ch:["N(t) = 500 · 2<sup>t/3</sup>","N(t) = 500 · 2<sup>3t</sup>","N(t) = 500 · 3<sup>t</sup>","N(t) = 500 · 2t/3"], a:0,
 e:"In 3 hours the population multiplies by 2, so after t hours it multiplies by 2 once per 3 hours — that is, 2<sup>t/3</sup>. Choice B would double every hour (a factor of 8 in 3 hours); C treats the base as 3 per hour; D is linear growth, which contradicts “doubles.”"},
{id:"ADV04", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A car purchased for $24,000 loses 15% of its value each year.",
 q:"Which function gives V, the car’s value in dollars, t years after purchase?",
 ch:["V(t) = 24,000(0.15)<sup>t</sup>","V(t) = 24,000(0.85)<sup>t</sup>","V(t) = 24,000(1.15)<sup>t</sup>","V(t) = 24,000 − 3,600t"], a:1,
 e:"Keeping 15%? No — losing 15% means keeping 85% each year, so the value multiplies by 0.85 every year: V(t) = 24,000(0.85)<sup>t</sup>. Choice A keeps only 15%, C models growth, and D is linear depreciation, not compounding loss."},
{id:"ADV05", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"y = x<sup>2</sup> − 4x + 5<br>y = 2x − 3",
 q:"The graphs of the two equations intersect at (x, y). What is one possible value of x?",
 ch:["3","2","8","−2"], a:1,
 e:"Set x<sup>2</sup> − 4x + 5 = 2x − 3 → x<sup>2</sup> − 6x + 8 = 0 → (x − 2)(x − 4) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 4. Choice A (3) is the vertex x of the parabola, a tempting nearby number; 8 and −2 are not roots."},
{id:"ADV06", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"If (x − 3) is a factor of x<sup>2</sup> + kx − 15, where k is a constant.",
 q:"What is the value of k?",
 ch:["−2","2","5","−5"], a:1,
 e:"If (x − 3) is a factor, then x = 3 makes the polynomial zero: 9 + 3k − 15 = 0 → 3k = 6 → k = 2. (Factor check: (x − 3)(x + 5) = x<sup>2</sup> + 2x − 15.) The wrong signs in the other options come from mixing up +3 and −3."},
{id:"ADV07", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"The graph of y = f(x) is transformed by shifting it 3 units to the right and 2 units up.",
 q:"Which equation represents the transformed graph?",
 ch:["y = f(x + 3) + 2","y = f(x − 3) + 2","y = f(x − 3) − 2","y = f(x) + 5"], a:1,
 e:"Right shift replaces x with x − 3; up shift adds 2 outside the function: y = f(x − 3) + 2. Choice A shifts left (the classic sign trap); C moves down instead of up; D adds both shifts’ magnitudes to the output, which ignores the horizontal move."},
{id:"ADV08", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"√(x<sup>6</sup>) / x<sup>2</sup>, where x &gt; 0",
 q:"Which expression is equivalent to the one shown above?",
 ch:["x<sup>2</sup>","x","x<sup>4</sup>","x<sup>3</sup>"], a:1,
 e:"√(x<sup>6</sup>) = x<sup>3</sup> (valid since x &gt; 0), and x<sup>3</sup>/x<sup>2</sup> = x. Choice D stops after the square root; A and B misapply the exponent rules; x<sup>4</sup> results from adding exponents instead of subtracting."},
{id:"ADV09", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"A population of beetles is counted each year:<br><table style='width:70%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.9rem'><tr style='color:var(--muted)'><th style='text-align:left;padding:4px'>Years since start (t)</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th></tr><tr><td style='padding:4px'>Beetles (N)</td><td style='text-align:center'>4</td><td style='text-align:center'>12</td><td style='text-align:center'>36</td><td style='text-align:center'>108</td></tr></table>",
 q:"Which type of function best models N as a function of t, and what is its equation?",
 ch:["Linear: N = 12t","Exponential: N = 4 · 3<sup>t</sup>","Exponential: N = 4t<sup>3</sup>","Linear: N = 4 + 108t"], a:1,
 e:"Each year the count multiplies by 3 (12/4 = 3, 36/12 = 3, 108/36 = 3): constant ratios mean exponential growth with base 3 and starting value 4. N = 4 · 3<sup>t</sup>. The linear fits fail at t = 0 (they must give 4) or at every later year."},
{id:"ADV10", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"x<sup>2</sup> + 10x + c = (x + 5)<sup>2</sup>, where c is a constant.",
 q:"What is the value of c?",
 ch:["10","25","5","100"], a:1,
 e:"(x + 5)<sup>2</sup> = x<sup>2</sup> + 10x + 25, so c = 25 — the square of half the middle coefficient. Choice A copies the 10; 100 squares the whole coefficient instead of half of it."},

/* ---------- Problem Solving & Data Analysis ---------- */
{id:"PSD01", d:"PSD", tier:"elite",
 p:"A jacket’s price increased by 25% and became $75. Later, the store applied a 20% discount to that new price.",
 q:"Compared with the jacket’s original price, what was the final price after the discount?",
 ch:["5% higher","Equal to the original price","5% lower","20% lower"], a:1,
 e:"Original = 75/1.25 = $60. Final = 75 × 0.80 = $60. A +25% rise and a −20% cut multiply to 1.25 × 0.80 = 1.00 — a full round trip. Percent changes compose by multiplication, not addition, which is exactly the trap the wrong options set."},
{id:"PSD02", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A pump fills 45 gallons in 6 minutes, working at a constant rate.",
 q:"At this rate, how many minutes does the pump take to fill 120 gallons?",
 ch:["18","16","13.5","20"], a:1,
 e:"Rate = 45/6 = 7.5 gallons per minute. Time = 120 ÷ 7.5 = 16 minutes. Choice A scales 6 minutes by 120/45 incorrectly (that quotient is not an integer of minutes); 13.5 inverts the ratio."},
{id:"PSD03", d:"PSD", tier:"elite",
 p:"A café surveyed 80 people about their drink orders:<br><table style='width:85%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.9rem'><tr style='color:var(--muted)'><th style='text-align:left;padding:4px'></th><th>Coffee</th><th>Tea</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td style='padding:4px'>Students</td><td style='text-align:center'>38</td><td style='text-align:center'>22</td><td style='text-align:center'>60</td></tr><tr><td style='padding:4px;background:var(--card2)'>Teachers</td><td style='text-align:center;background:var(--card2)'>12</td><td style='text-align:center;background:var(--card2)'>8</td><td style='text-align:center;background:var(--card2)'>20</td></tr><tr><td style='padding:4px'>Total</td><td style='text-align:center'>50</td><td style='text-align:center'>30</td><td style='text-align:center'>80</td></tr></table>",
 q:"One person is chosen at random from all the people who chose tea. What is the probability that the person is a teacher?",
 ch:["1/10","2/5","4/15","3/8"], a:2,
 e:"“Chosen from all who chose tea” restricts the denominator to the tea row total: 30 people. Teachers among them: 8. Probability = 8/30 = 4/15. Choice A (8/80) forgets the condition; B (8/20) uses the wrong row; D (30/80) is the probability of tea overall."},
{id:"PSD04", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The mean of five numbers is 14. Four of the numbers are 12, 15, 11, and 16.",
 q:"What is the fifth number?",
 ch:["14","16","12","18"], a:1,
 e:"Total = 5 × 14 = 70. The four known numbers sum to 12 + 15 + 11 + 16 = 54. The fifth is 70 − 54 = 16. Choice A echoes the mean itself — a classic bait."},
{id:"PSD05", d:"PSD", tier:"elite",
 p:"A data set consists of 3, 5, 7, 9, 21.",
 q:"Adding which single value to the data set increases its median while leaving its mean unchanged?",
 ch:["7","9","21","3"], a:1,
 e:"Current mean = 45/5 = 9; adding 9 keeps the mean at 54/6 = 9. The new ordered set is 3, 5, 7, 9, 9, 21, whose median is (7 + 9)/2 = 8 — up from 7. Adding 7 or 3 lowers the mean; adding 21 raises it, and none of those moves the median upward."},
{id:"PSD06", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The line of best fit for a scatterplot is y = 3.2x + 14, where x is grams of fertilizer applied to a plot and y is the predicted harvest in kilograms.",
 q:"What does the number 3.2 represent in this context?",
 ch:["The predicted harvest with no fertilizer","The predicted increase in harvest, in kilograms, for each additional gram of fertilizer","The total amount of fertilizer applied","The number of plots in the study"], a:1,
 e:"The slope is the per-unit change in the prediction: +3.2 kg of harvest per extra gram of fertilizer. The 14 is the predicted harvest at x = 0 (choice A); choices C and D assign a slope-like number to quantities it does not measure."},
{id:"PSD07", d:"PSD", tier:"elite",
 p:"At a school with 1,200 students, 35% walk to school. Of the students who walk, 60% walk more than one mile.",
 q:"How many students walk to school for more than one mile?",
 ch:["420","252","720","180"], a:1,
 e:"Walkers: 1,200 × 0.35 = 420. More than one mile: 420 × 0.60 = 252. Choice A stops after the first percentage; C applies 60% to the whole school (1,200 × 0.60 = 720); D mis-scales the walker count."},
{id:"PSD08", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A soup recipe calls for flour and water in a ratio of 5 : 2 by volume.",
 q:"If a cook uses 350 milliliters of flour, how many milliliters of water are needed?",
 ch:["100","140","175","875"], a:1,
 e:"Water = (2/5) × flour = (2/5) × 350 = 140 mL. Choice A uses a 1 : 3.5 slip; 875 multiplies by 5/2, reversing the ratio (that would be flour needed for 350 mL of water)."},
{id:"PSD09", d:"PSD", tier:"elite",
 p:"Two data sets each have a mean of 7.<br>Set A: 4, 4, 4, 16&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Set B: 6, 7, 8, 9",
 q:"Which statement about the standard deviations of the two sets is true?",
 ch:["Set B has the larger standard deviation because its values are more evenly spaced","Set A has the larger standard deviation because its values are farther from the mean","The two sets have the same standard deviation because they have the same mean","Standard deviation cannot be computed for either set"], a:1,
 e:"Standard deviation measures spread about the mean. Set A’s values sit 3 units from the mean on average, while Set B’s sit only about 1 unit away — so A’s standard deviation is larger despite identical means. Equal means say nothing about spread, and four values are plenty for computing an SD."},
{id:"PSD10", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A student planned to read a 400-page document. On Monday the student read 30% of the pages, and on Tuesday the student read 60% of the pages that remained.",
 q:"How many pages remained unread after Tuesday?",
 ch:["160","112","280","88"], a:1,
 e:"Monday leaves 70% of 400 = 280 pages. Tuesday removes 60% of those, leaving 40%: 280 × 0.40 = 112 pages. Choice A wrongly takes 60% of 280 as the remainder; C stops after Monday; D misapplies 30% twice."},

/* ---------- Geometry & Trigonometry ---------- */
{id:"GEO01", d:"GEO", tier:"elite",
 p:"A circle in the xy-plane has its center at (−2, 5) and a radius of 3.",
 q:"Which equation represents this circle?",
 ch:["(x − 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y + 5)<sup>2</sup> = 9","(x + 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 9","(x + 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 3","(x − 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y + 5)<sup>2</sup> = 6"], a:1,
 e:"Standard form is (x − h)<sup>2</sup> + (y − k)<sup>2</sup> = r<sup>2</sup> with center (h, k). Here h = −2, k = 5, r<sup>2</sup> = 9: (x + 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 9. Choice A flips both signs (that circle is centered at (2, −5)); C forgets to square the radius; D confuses diameter (6) with r<sup>2</sup>."},
{id:"GEO02", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"In a right triangle, the hypotenuse is 10 and one acute angle measures 30°.",
 q:"What is the length of the shorter leg?",
 ch:["5","5√3","10","10√3"], a:0,
 e:"In a 30°–60°–90° triangle the leg opposite the 30° angle is half the hypotenuse: 10/2 = 5. The longer leg is 5√3 — choice B is the longer leg, not the shorter."},
{id:"GEO03", d:"GEO", tier:"elite",
 p:"On level ground, a person 1.8 meters tall casts a shadow 1.2 meters long. At the same moment, a tower casts a shadow 40 meters long.",
 q:"What is the height of the tower, in meters?",
 ch:["54","60","66.7","72"], a:1,
 e:"Similar triangles: height/shadow is constant. 1.8/1.2 = 1.5, so the tower’s height = 1.5 × 40 = 60 m. Choice C divides the other way and rounds; A and D scramble the ratio."},
{id:"GEO04", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The measures of the angles of a triangle are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4.",
 q:"What is the measure, in degrees, of the largest angle?",
 ch:["80","90","60","100"], a:0,
 e:"The parts total 2 + 3 + 4 = 9, and the angles sum to 180°, so each part is 20°. The largest angle is 4 × 20° = 80°. The other options come from using 2 or 3 parts, or summing past 180°."},
{id:"GEO05", d:"GEO", tier:"elite",
 p:"A circle has radius 6. A sector of the circle has a central angle of 60°.",
 q:"What is the area of the sector, in square units?",
 ch:["12π","6π","3π","36π"], a:1,
 e:"Sector area = (central angle/360) × πr<sup>2</sup> = (60/360) × 36π = 6π. Choice A treats the fraction as 1/3 instead of 1/6; C halves again; D is the full circle’s area."},
{id:"GEO06", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A cylinder has volume V. A similar cylinder is formed by doubling the radius while keeping the height the same.",
 q:"What is the volume of the new cylinder in terms of V?",
 ch:["2V","4V","8V","16V"], a:1,
 e:"Volume scales with the square of a linear dimension: V = πr<sup>2</sup>h, so doubling r multiplies volume by 2<sup>2</sup> = 4. Choice C (8V) applies the cube rule that only holds when all dimensions scale."},
{id:"GEO07", d:"GEO", tier:"elite",
 p:"For an acute angle θ, sin θ = 0.6.",
 q:"What is cos θ?",
 ch:["0.8","0.6","0.4","1.2"], a:0,
 e:"sin<sup>2</sup>θ + cos<sup>2</sup>θ = 1, so cos θ = √(1 − 0.36) = √0.64 = 0.8 for an acute angle. Choice B copies the sine; 1.2 is impossible — a cosine can never exceed 1."},
{id:"GEO08", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A 13-meter ladder leans against a vertical wall, with its base 5 meters from the wall on level ground.",
 q:"How high up the wall does the ladder reach, in meters?",
 ch:["12","8","18","13"], a:0,
 e:"Right triangle with hypotenuse 13 and one leg 5: height = √(13<sup>2</sup> − 5<sup>2</sup>) = √(169 − 25) = √144 = 12. Choice B subtracts instead of squaring; 18 adds; 13 ignores the geometry."},
{id:"GEO09", d:"GEO", tier:"elite",
 p:"The endpoints of a circle’s diameter are (1, −2) and (7, 4).",
 q:"What are the coordinates of the center of the circle?",
 ch:["(3, 1)","(4, 1)","(8, 2)","(4, −1)"], a:1,
 e:"The center is the midpoint of the diameter: ((1 + 7)/2, (−2 + 4)/2) = (4, 1). Choice A averages one coordinate and subtracts the other; D mixes the order of the two points’ coordinates."},
{id:"GEO10", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"In a right triangle, the two acute angles measure (3x)° and (2x + 10)°.",
 q:"What is the value of x?",
 ch:["16","18","15","26"], a:0,
 e:"Acute angles of a right triangle sum to 90°: 3x + 2x + 10 = 90 → 5x = 80 → x = 16. (Check: 48° + 42° = 90°.) Choice D sums to 180° as if the triangle had no right angle."},
{id:"ALG11", d:"ALG", tier:"core",
 p:"An electrician charges a fixed call-out fee plus an hourly rate. The total cost, C, in dollars, for a job that takes h hours is given by C = 45h + 90.",
 q:"What does the number 90 represent in this equation?",
 ch:["The cost of a two-hour job", "The number of hours worked", "The fixed call-out fee charged before any hourly work", "The hourly rate"], a:2,
 e:"In C = 45h + 90, the constant 90 is the starting value — the fee before any hours are worked. Choice D confuses it with the slope 45; Choice A evaluates the wrong quantity (a two-hour job costs 45(2) + 90 = 180); Choice B describes h, which has no fixed value."},
{id:"ALG12", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"3(x − 4) = 5x + 2",
 q:"What is the value of x?",
 ch:["7", "−5", "5", "−7"], a:3,
 e:"Distribute: 3x − 12 = 5x + 2, so −14 = 2x and x = −7. Choice A drops the negative; Choices B and C come from sign slips when moving terms across the equals sign."},
{id:"ALG13", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"Two years from now, Ana will be twice as old as her cousin Ben. Two years ago, Ana was three times as old as Ben.",
 q:"How old is Ana now?",
 ch:["14", "12", "16", "10"], a:0,
 e:"Let a and b be their current ages: a + 2 = 2(b + 2) and a − 2 = 3(b − 2). Subtracting the equations gives 4 = −b + 8, so b = 6 and a = 14. Check: in two years, 16 vs 8; two years ago, 12 vs 4. Choice B is Ben’s age; Choices C and D come from sign errors in setting up the equations."},
{id:"ALG14", d:"ALG", tier:"core",
 p:"A rideshare driver aims to earn at least $180 in a shift. The driver makes $4 for each trip plus $0.80 for each mile driven.",
 q:"Which inequality represents the number of trips t and miles m that meet the goal?",
 ch:["4 + 0.80(t + m) ≥ 180", "4t + 0.80m ≥ 180", "4t + 0.80m ≤ 180", "0.80t + 4m ≥ 180"], a:1,
 e:"“At least” means ≥, and each trip contributes $4 while each mile contributes $0.80: 4t + 0.80m ≥ 180. Choice C reverses the inequality; Choice D swaps the two rates; Choice A folds the per-trip amount into a single sum."},
{id:"ALG15", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"A water tank drains at a constant rate. Three hours after draining begins, the tank holds 9,200 liters; seven hours after it begins, it holds 6,800 liters.",
 q:"How many liters did the tank hold at the moment draining began?",
 ch:["8,000", "10,400", "11,000", "12,200"], a:2,
 e:"The rate is (6,800 − 9,200)/(7 − 3) = −600 liters per hour, so at hour 0 the tank held 9,200 + 3(600) = 11,000 liters. Choice B adds back only two hours of drainage; Choice D and Choice A come from misreading the rate or averaging the readings."},
{id:"ALG16", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The perimeter of a rectangle is P = 2w + 2ℓ, where w is the width and ℓ is the length.",
 q:"Which equation gives ℓ in terms of P and w?",
 ch:["ℓ = P − 2w", "ℓ = (P − w)/2", "ℓ = 2P − 2w", "ℓ = (P − 2w)/2"], a:3,
 e:"Subtract 2w from both sides: P − 2w = 2ℓ, then divide by 2. Choice A forgets the division; Choice B subtracts only half of what is needed before dividing; Choice C reverses the operations entirely."},
{id:"ALG17", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"A theater sold 220 tickets for one show. Adult tickets cost $14 and student tickets cost $8, and total receipts were $2,360.",
 q:"How many adult tickets were sold?",
 ch:["100", "120", "80", "110"], a:0,
 e:"With s = 220 − a: 14a + 8(220 − a) = 2,360, so 6a = 600 and a = 100. Choice C answers for students; Choices B and D come from arithmetic slips in combining the ticket revenue."},
{id:"ALG18", d:"ALG", tier:"core",
 p:"The value V, in dollars, of a company’s equipment t years after purchase is V = 24,000 − 1,600t.",
 q:"Which statement about the equipment is true?",
 ch:["Its value increases by $24,000 each year", "It loses $1,600 in value each year", "It was worth $1,600 when purchased", "After 15 years it will be worth twice its purchase price"], a:1,
 e:"The slope −1,600 is the yearly loss, and 24,000 is the purchase value. At t = 15, V = 0 — worthless, not doubled — and the value decreases, so Choices C, D, and A all misread the equation."},
{id:"ALG19", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A recipe calls for 2/3 cup of oats per serving. A chef wants to make 9 servings but has already measured out 4 cups of oats.",
 q:"How many more cups of oats does the chef need?",
 ch:["6", "1", "2", "3"], a:2,
 e:"Nine servings require 9 × 2/3 = 6 cups, and 6 − 4 = 2 more. Choice A forgets the 4 cups already measured; Choices D and B come from misapplying the fraction."},
{id:"ALG20", d:"ALG", tier:"elite",
 p:"A generator starts with a full 60-liter fuel tank and burns fuel at a constant rate. The graph of the fuel remaining F, in liters, versus time t, in hours, is a line with a vertical intercept of 60 and a horizontal intercept of 10.",
 q:"What does the horizontal intercept t = 10 represent?",
 ch:["The number of liters burned per hour", "The size of the fuel tank", "The time at which the tank is half full", "The number of hours the generator can run before the tank is empty"], a:3,
 e:"F = 0 when t = 10 — the tank empties after 10 hours of running. Choice A describes the slope (6 liters per hour); Choice B is the vertical intercept; Choice C would be the time when F = 30, not 0."},
{id:"ALG21", d:"ALG", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Membership at Pool A costs $40 per month with no starting fee. Pool B costs $28 per month plus a $144 starting fee. Both charge the same total after a certain number of months.",
 q:"After how many months do the two pools cost the same in total?",
 ch:["12", "144", "6", "18"], a:0,
 e:"Set 40t = 28t + 144: subtracting 28t gives 12t = 144, so t = 12 months. Choice B forgets to divide by the monthly difference; Choices C and D come from miscomputing 144 ÷ 12."},
{id:"ADV11", d:"ADV", tier:"core",
 p:"A gym charges a $75 enrollment fee and $32 per month.",
 q:"Which equation gives the total cost C, in dollars, of the gym after m months?",
 ch:["C = 107m", "C = 75 + 32m", "C = 32 + 75m", "C = 75m + 32m"], a:1,
 e:"The one-time fee plus the monthly rate times the number of months: C = 75 + 32m. Choice C swaps the roles of fee and rate; Choices D and A misread which charge is monthly."},
{id:"ADV12", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"(3x<sup>2</sup>y)<sup>3</sup> · (2xy<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup>",
 q:"Which expression is equivalent to the one above?",
 ch:["108x<sup>7</sup>y<sup>6</sup>", "6x<sup>8</sup>y<sup>7</sup>", "108x<sup>8</sup>y<sup>7</sup>", "72x<sup>8</sup>y<sup>7</sup>"], a:2,
 e:"Cubing gives 27x<sup>6</sup>y<sup>3</sup>; squaring gives 4x<sup>2</sup>y<sup>4</sup>; multiplying gives 108x<sup>8</sup>y<sup>7</sup>. Choice D mishandles the coefficient powers; Choice A adds the wrong exponent pairs; Choice B merely multiplies the original bases, 3 × 2."},
{id:"ADV13", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"If (x − 4) is a factor of the polynomial x<sup>2</sup> − 11x + k, where k is a constant.",
 q:"What is the value of k?",
 ch:["−28", "44", "7", "28"], a:3,
 e:"A factor (x − 4) means x = 4 is a root: 16 − 44 + k = 0, so k = 28. (Indeed x<sup>2</sup> − 11x + 28 = (x − 4)(x − 7).) Choice A drops the sign; Choice B multiplies 4 by 11; Choice C confuses k with the other factor’s constant."},
{id:"ADV14", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"A colony of bacteria starts with 400 cells and triples in size every 2 hours.",
 q:"Which equation gives the number of cells N after t hours?",
 ch:["N = 400 · 3<sup>t/2</sup>", "N = 400 · 3<sup>2t</sup>", "N = 400 · (1/3)<sup>t/2</sup>", "N = 1,200t"], a:0,
 e:"In t hours there are t/2 triplings, so N = 400 · 3<sup>t/2</sup>. Choice B triples every half-hour instead of every two hours; Choice C models decay; Choice D is linear growth."},
{id:"ADV15", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Let f(x) = 2x<sup>2</sup> − 3 and g(x) = x + 5.",
 q:"What is the value of f(g(1))?",
 ch:["39", "69", "72", "4"], a:1,
 e:"Work inside out: g(1) = 6, then f(6) = 2(36) − 3 = 69. Choice C drops the −3; Choice D computes g(f(1)) instead, which gives 4; Choice A mis-evaluates the square."},
{id:"ADV16", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"The line y = 2x + 1 and the parabola y = x<sup>2</sup> − 2 are graphed in the xy-plane.",
 q:"At how many points do the two graphs intersect?",
 ch:["None", "Infinitely many", "Two", "One"], a:2,
 e:"Set x<sup>2</sup> − 2 = 2x + 1: x<sup>2</sup> − 2x − 3 = 0, which factors as (x − 3)(x + 1) — two real intersections. One point would mean tangency; none would require a negative discriminant; infinitely many would require identical graphs."},
{id:"ADV17", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"√(81x<sup>4</sup>)",
 q:"Which expression is equivalent to the one above?",
 ch:["81x<sup>2</sup>", "9x<sup>4</sup>", "3x<sup>2</sup>", "9x<sup>2</sup>"], a:3,
 e:"Take the square root of each factor: √81 · √(x<sup>4</sup>) = 9x<sup>2</sup>. Choice A leaves the coefficient unrooted; Choice B leaves the exponent unhalved; Choice C halving 81 instead of taking its root."},
{id:"ADV18", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"A market stall’s profit from selling n hand warmers in a day is modeled by P(n) = −2n<sup>2</sup> + 120n − 500, in dollars.",
 q:"How many hand warmers does the stall need to sell to maximize its daily profit?",
 ch:["30", "60", "24", "500"], a:0,
 e:"The parabola’s vertex lies at n = −b/(2a) = −120/(2 · (−2)) = 30. Choice B halves only once; Choice C misreads a coefficient; Choice D quotes the constant term rather than solving."},
{id:"ADV19", d:"ADV", tier:"core",
 p:"A car’s resale value is modeled by V = 22,000(0.85)<sup>t</sup>, where V is in dollars and t is the number of years since purchase.",
 q:"What does the number 0.85 represent in this model?",
 ch:["The number of years until the car loses half its value", "The car keeps 85 percent of its value each year", "The car loses 85 percent of its value each year", "The car’s value after one year"], a:1,
 e:"Multiplying by 0.85 each year keeps 85 percent — a 15 percent annual loss. Choice C reverses the meaning of the factor; Choice D confuses a multiplier with a value; Choice A is not represented anywhere in the model."},
{id:"ADV20", d:"ADV", tier:"elite",
 p:"5/x = x − 4",
 q:"What is the solution set of this equation?",
 ch:["x = −1 only", "x = 4", "x = 5 and x = −1", "x = 5 only"], a:2,
 e:"Multiply both sides by x: 5 = x<sup>2</sup> − 4x, so x<sup>2</sup> − 4x − 5 = 0 and (x − 5)(x + 1) = 0. Both roots are valid since neither makes x = 0. Choices D and A each drop a root; Choice B substitutes rather than solves."},
{id:"ADV21", d:"ADV", tier:"advanced",
 p:"The graph of y = (x − 3)<sup>2</sup> + 7 is a translation of the graph of y = x<sup>2</sup>.",
 q:"Which transformation produces the new graph from the original?",
 ch:["A shift left 3 units and up 7 units", "A shift right 7 units and up 3 units", "A shift right 3 units and down 7 units", "A shift right 3 units and up 7 units"], a:3,
 e:"Inside the square, (x − 3) shifts the graph right 3 — opposite the sign — and the +7 shifts it up. Choices A and C reverse directions; Choice B swaps the two distances."},
{id:"PSD11", d:"PSD", tier:"core",
 p:"A town’s population grew from 24,000 to 27,600 over a decade.",
 q:"What was the percent increase in the town’s population?",
 ch:["15%", "13%", "87.5%", "115%"], a:0,
 e:"The increase is 3,600 people, and 3,600/24,000 = 0.15, or 15%. Choice B divides by the new population; Choice C is the old-to-new ratio read backwards; Choice D is the new population as a percent of the old."},
{id:"PSD12", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Of 80 volunteers, 45 preferred morning shifts and 35 preferred evening shifts. Of the 45 morning-preferring volunteers, 30 were students; of the evening-preferring volunteers, 10 were students.",
 q:"If one volunteer is chosen at random, what is the probability that the volunteer is a student who prefers evening shifts?",
 ch:["1/2", "1/8", "2/7", "10/45"], a:1,
 e:"Evening-preferring students number 10 out of 80 total volunteers: 10/80 = 1/8. Choice C conditions on the evening group (10/35); Choice D conditions on the wrong row (10/45); Choice A counts all students, 40/80."},
{id:"PSD13", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A team’s five salaries, in thousands of dollars, are 52, 55, 57, 58, and 240.",
 q:"Which statement about the data is true?",
 ch:["The mean and the median are equal", "The median cannot be computed from five values", "The mean is greater than the median because the largest salary pulls it up", "The median is greater than the mean because salaries cluster low"], a:2,
 e:"The mean is (52 + 55 + 57 + 58 + 240)/5 = 92.4, while the median is 57 — the outlier drags the mean far above the median. The other choices reverse the relationship, deny the gap, or claim a median cannot be found."},
{id:"PSD14", d:"PSD", tier:"elite",
 p:"A poll of 400 randomly selected voters finds 54 percent support for a bond measure, with a margin of error of 4.9 percentage points.",
 q:"Which conclusion is best supported by the poll?",
 ch:["Exactly 54 percent of all voters support the measure", "The measure will certainly fail if support is below 50 percent", "The poll overrepresents younger voters", "Between roughly 49.1 and 58.9 percent of all voters likely support the measure"], a:3,
 e:"The margin of error brackets plausible true support at 54 ± 4.9 — roughly 49.1 to 58.9 percent. Exact values and certainty go beyond what a sample allows, and nothing in the poll’s description concerns age composition."},
{id:"PSD15", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A study of 30 towns found that miles of bike lane per resident, b, predicted shop revenue per resident, R, in hundreds of dollars, according to R = 1.8b + 4.2.",
 q:"What does the slope 1.8 suggest in this context?",
 ch:["Each additional mile of bike lane per resident is associated with about $180 more shop revenue per resident", "Each additional resident adds 1.8 miles of bike lane", "Towns with no bike lanes have no shop revenue at all", "Shop revenue causes towns to build bike lanes"], a:0,
 e:"The slope links each extra bike-lane mile per resident to 1.8 hundred dollars — about $180 — of additional revenue per resident. Choice B reverses the variables; Choice C misreads the intercept 4.2; Choice D turns an association into a causal claim."},
{id:"PSD16", d:"PSD", tier:"core",
 p:"In a class of 60 students, 24 take Spanish and 36 take French. Among the Spanish takers, 15 also take French.",
 q:"If a student is chosen at random from the French takers, what is the probability that the student also takes Spanish?",
 ch:["2/5", "5/12", "1/4", "15/60"], a:1,
 e:"Condition on the French group: 15 of the 36 French takers also take Spanish, and 15/36 = 5/12. Choice C divides by the whole class; Choice D is the unconditional probability; Choice A is 24/60, the chance of Spanish overall."},
{id:"PSD17", d:"PSD", tier:"elite",
 p:"For 14 rivers, researchers plotted dam height, in meters, against downstream summer water temperature, in degrees Celsius. The points drift steadily downward from the upper left to the lower right of the plot, with a few scattered exceptions.",
 q:"Which statement best describes the association shown?",
 ch:["No association, because a few points break the pattern", "A causal effect of water temperature on dam height", "A strong negative association: taller dams tend to pair with cooler downstream temperatures", "A strong positive association between dam height and temperature"], a:2,
 e:"A steady downward drift is strong and negative; a handful of exceptions do not erase it. Choice A overweights the exceptions, and an association — however strong — cannot by itself establish that temperature builds dams."},
{id:"PSD18", d:"PSD", tier:"core",
 p:"A machine prints 640 labels in 8 minutes, working at a constant rate.",
 q:"At this rate, how many minutes will it take the machine to print 1,200 labels?",
 ch:["12", "16", "9.6", "15"], a:3,
 e:"The rate is 640 ÷ 8 = 80 labels per minute, so 1,200 ÷ 80 = 15 minutes. Choices A, B, and C come from assuming rates of 100, 75, and 125 labels per minute."},
{id:"PSD19", d:"PSD", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A researcher wants to estimate the average commute time of all workers in a large city.",
 q:"Which method is most likely to produce a valid estimate?",
 ch:["Randomly sampling workers from complete city employment records and measuring their commutes", "Asking commuters waiting at the central station at 8 a.m.", "Posting an online survey and using the first 500 responses", "Measuring traffic density on the city’s widest highway"], a:0,
 e:"A random sample drawn from a complete roster supports generalization to all workers. Station waiters oversample peak-hour transit users, online responders are self-selected, and traffic density does not measure commute time at all."},
{id:"PSD20", d:"PSD", tier:"elite",
 p:"A course grade is weighted: homework counts 40 percent and exams count 60 percent. A student averages 92 on homework and 78 on exams.",
 q:"What is the student’s final course grade?",
 ch:["81.2", "83.6", "85", "86.8"], a:1,
 e:"0.40(92) + 0.60(78) = 36.8 + 46.8 = 83.6. Choice C averages the two scores as if they counted equally; Choices D and A swap or drop the weights."},
{id:"PSD21", d:"PSD", tier:"core",
 p:"The ages of seven people at a workshop are 12, 15, 15, 19, 21, 24, and 40. An eighth person, age 16, joins the group.",
 q:"What is the median age of the eight people?",
 ch:["16", "21", "17.5", "19"], a:2,
 e:"Sorted, the eight ages are 12, 15, 15, 16, 19, 21, 24, 40. The median of eight values is the average of the 4th and 5th: (16 + 19)/2 = 17.5. Choice D keeps the old median; Choice A reports only the newcomer’s age; Choice B reports the 5th value alone."},
{id:"GEO11", d:"GEO", tier:"core",
 p:"A 6-meter ladder leans against a wall, making a 65° angle with the ground.",
 q:"Which expression gives the height the ladder reaches on the wall, in meters?",
 ch:["6 cos 65°", "6/sin 65°", "sin 65°/6", "6 sin 65°"], a:3,
 e:"The height on the wall is opposite the 65° angle, and the ladder is the hypotenuse: sin 65° = h/6, so h = 6 sin 65°. Choice A uses the adjacent-side ratio; Choices B and C invert the definition of sine."},
{id:"GEO12", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A circle in the xy-plane has center (−2, 5) and radius 6.",
 q:"Which equation represents this circle?",
 ch:["(x + 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 36", "(x − 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y + 5)<sup>2</sup> = 36", "(x + 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 6", "(x − 2)<sup>2</sup> + (y + 5)<sup>2</sup> = 12"], a:0,
 e:"A center of (−2, 5) flips the signs inside the parentheses, and r<sup>2</sup> = 36. Choice B flips the center’s coordinates; Choice C forgets to square the radius; Choice D mishandles both."},
{id:"GEO13", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"At the same time of day, a person 1.8 meters tall casts a 2.4-meter shadow, and a nearby flagpole casts a 16-meter shadow.",
 q:"How tall is the flagpole, in meters?",
 ch:["13.6", "12", "21.3", "10.8"], a:1,
 e:"Similar triangles give 1.8/2.4 = h/16, so h = 1.8 × 16/2.4 = 12 meters. Choice C inverts the ratio; Choices D and A come from slips in the multiplication."},
{id:"GEO14", d:"GEO", tier:"core",
 p:"A cylindrical storage tank has a radius of 3 meters and a height of 10 meters.",
 q:"Which expression gives the volume of the tank, in cubic meters?",
 ch:["60π", "9π", "90π", "30π"], a:2,
 e:"V = πr<sup>2</sup>h = π(3<sup>2</sup>)(10) = 90π. Choice D forgets to square the radius; Choice A doubles it instead; Choice B stops after squaring."},
{id:"GEO15", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One of the angles formed measures 112°.",
 q:"What is the measure of the angle that is alternate interior to the 112° angle?",
 ch:["68°", "248°", "90°", "112°"], a:3,
 e:"Alternate interior angles between parallel lines are congruent, so the answer is 112°. Choice A gives the supplementary angle, which belongs to the same-side interior pair; the others invent angle sums."},
{id:"GEO16", d:"GEO", tier:"elite",
 p:"A rectangular box has a square base with sides of 6 centimeters and a height of 8 centimeters.",
 q:"What is the length, in centimeters, of the longest segment that fits entirely inside the box?",
 ch:["2√34", "10", "12", "14"], a:0,
 e:"The space diagonal satisfies d<sup>2</sup> = 6<sup>2</sup> + 6<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> = 136, so d = √136 = 2√34 ≈ 11.7 cm. Choice 10 ignores one base dimension; Choices 12 and 14 guess round lengths without computing."},
{id:"GEO17", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"In triangle ABC, the measure of angle A is 35°, and angle B measures 20° more than angle C.",
 q:"What is the measure of angle C?",
 ch:["72.5°", "62.5°", "65°", "55°"], a:1,
 e:"Angle sum: 35 + (C + 20) + C = 180, so 2C = 125 and C = 62.5°. Choice C ignores the extra 20°; Choices D and A misassign which angle gets the surplus."},
{id:"GEO18", d:"GEO", tier:"elite",
 p:"A circle has center O. Line t is tangent to the circle at point P. Q is a point on line t with PQ = 8 cm, and OQ = 10 cm.",
 q:"What is the radius of the circle, in centimeters?",
 ch:["8", "√164", "6", "10"], a:2,
 e:"A radius drawn to a tangent point is perpendicular to the tangent line, so triangle OPQ is right-angled at P: OP<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>2</sup> − 8<sup>2</sup> = 36, giving OP = 6. Choice D hands back the hypotenuse; Choice A the given leg; Choice B adds instead of subtracting."},
{id:"GEO19", d:"GEO", tier:"core",
 p:"Points A and B are plotted in the xy-plane at (−3, 1) and (5, −5).",
 q:"What is the distance between points A and B?",
 ch:["14", "√14", "100", "10"], a:3,
 e:"√((5 − (−3))<sup>2</sup> + (−5 − 1)<sup>2</sup>) = √(64 + 36) = √100 = 10. Choice A adds the coordinate differences; Choice B forgets to square them; Choice C stops before taking the square root."},
{id:"GEO20", d:"GEO", tier:"elite",
 p:"A circular garden has a radius of 9 meters. A wedge of the garden with a 60° central angle is planted with lavender.",
 q:"What is the area of the lavender wedge, in square meters?",
 ch:["13.5π", "27π", "81π", "4.5π"], a:0,
 e:"A 60° sector is 60/360 = 1/6 of the circle: (1/6)π(9<sup>2</sup>) = 81π/6 = 13.5π. Choice B treats the wedge as a third of the circle; Choice C reports the whole garden; Choice D takes a twelfth."},
{id:"GEO21", d:"GEO", tier:"advanced",
 p:"A triangle has an area of 24 square centimeters. A similar triangle is drawn with every side length multiplied by 3.",
 q:"What is the area of the larger triangle, in square centimeters?",
 ch:["48", "216", "72", "8"], a:1,
 e:"Area scales as the square of the scale factor: 24 × 3<sup>2</sup> = 24 × 9 = 216. Choice C applies the factor once; Choice D divides by it; Choice A doubles instead of squaring."},
];
